ml', 'snippet': 'This summer, small, portable suitcases in bright colors and luxe textures feel polished and unfussy — and will set you apart at check-in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer, small, portable suitcases in bright colors and luxe textures feel polished and unfussy — and will set you apart at check-in.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trunk Worth Bringing as Carry-On', 'kicker': 'Market Report', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-01T04:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Renata Mosci', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Renata', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mosci', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9917284a-6427-51e2-8654-c49ad2f82f09', 'word_count': 23, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9917284a-6427-51e2-8654-c49ad2f82f09'}, {'abstract': 'In February 2012, CNN severed its relationship with the host; now, at 81, he’s as comfortable with the brave new wired world as any millennial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/01/t-magazine/larry-king-internet-afterlife.html', 'snippet': 'In February 2012, CNN severed its relationship with the host; now, at 81, he’s as comfortable with the brave new wired world as any millennial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Larry King has looked like a retiree for at least three decades — like the snappiest dresser at a Boca Raton assisted-living facility — but he only stopped working in 2012, and not by choice. First, in 2010, King was eased out of his post at “Larry King Live,” the nightly talk show he had hosted on CNN since 1985. Then, in February 2012, CNN severed the relationship entirely, announcing that King would no longer appear even in occasional specials on the network. King was 78 years old, but he was worse at being retired than Brett Favre or Jay Z. Exactly a month later came the news that King would be returning to talk show hosting, though not, strictly speaking, to the airwaves. He co-founded a new production company, Ora TV, with the Mexican tycoon Carlos Slim Helú (who also has an ownership stake in The New York Times), and signed a multiyear deal for a new web series, “Larry King Now,” with the streaming site Hulu.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30king-rosen-1/30king-rosen-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'widt