: 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-01T13:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/615719f9-2211-5d3a-bec5-533b34802504', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/615719f9-2211-5d3a-bec5-533b34802504'}, {'abstract': 'The model and actress, who was recently named Jo Malone’s first-ever brand ambassador, shares her seasonal beauty tips.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/01/model-poppy-delevingne-beauty-secrets/', 'snippet': 'The model and actress, who was recently named Jo Malone’s first-ever brand ambassador, shares her seasonal beauty tips.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the English model and actress Poppy Delevingne, smelling good is every bit as important as looking good. “All my life, I’ve always been very into scent; I’m very sensitive to it,” she said earlier this month from London, where she was busy packing for the Glastonbury Festival. “I can literally meet someone in a pub and be like, ‘You smell like my best friend,’ and know exactly what the scent is.” Delevingne is especially fond of the aromas of popcorn and jasmine, which she says evoke memories of her childhood. But when it comes to her own signature scent, she has long been loyal to the British perfume house Jo Malone, whose Red Roses has been her everyday perfume since her early 20s. And the house recently named Delevingne its first-ever brand ambassador. “I’ve gotten into layering the scents, kind of like being a chemist,” she says. “I’ve been wearing a lot of the Peony & Blush Suede; at night I add Tuberose Angelica to make it a little muskier, a little sexier. And lately I’m really into Wood Sage & Sea Salt. It reminds you literally of the sea, so I’ve been wearing that for the summer.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/30/t-magazine/30beddie-poppy/30beddie-poppy-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poppy Delevingne’s Secrets for Staying, and Looking, Cool This Summer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rules of Style: Coming Up Roses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-01T13:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81b9e50c-ab08-57a5-91d1-0db4c8e1ee0e', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81b9e50c-ab08-57a5-91d1-0db4c8e1ee0e'}, {'abstract': 'The deal will combine two large providers of property and casualty insurance and comes as insurers are looking to increase their scale and product mix.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/02/business/dealbook/ace-to-buy-chubb-for-28-3-billion.html', 'snippet': 'The deal will combine two large providers of property and casualty insurance and comes as insurers are looking to increase their scale and product mix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Merger mania has swept across huge swaths of the corporate landscape in the last five years. Now the insurance industry, spurred by a $28.3 billion deal, could be about to join that wave.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/business/02CHUBB/02CHUBB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/02/business/02CHUBB/02CHUBB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/business/02CHUBB/02CHUBB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 