: None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f74e62aa-9c25-5a51-811d-04ba233eb82c', 'word_count': 51, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f74e62aa-9c25-5a51-811d-04ba233eb82c'}, {'abstract': 'Love announced on Wednesday that he would return to Cleveland to complete some “unfinished business,” while Anthony Davis and Jimmy Butler also reached new deals with their teams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/02/sports/basketball/nba-free-agency-roundup.html', 'snippet': 'Love announced on Wednesday that he would return to Cleveland to complete some “unfinished business,” while Anthony Davis and Jimmy Butler also reached new deals with their teams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The early hours of N.B.A. free agency on Wednesday featured plenty of lucrative deals without much player movement. Several stars, including Cleveland’s Kevin Love, opted to stay with their teams, which chose to spend freely as the league waded into a new era of inflated payrolls.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 702, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1404, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/sports/02nba-1/02nba-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cavaliers’ Kevin Love Is Among Stars Staying Put as Free Agency Begins', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love Is Staying With Cavs; Pierce Is Expected to Join Clippers, Reuniting With Rivers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Love, Kevin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Free Agents (Sports)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butler, Jimmy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Cavaliers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Anthony (1993- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-01T17:51:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Cacciola', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cacciola', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/478ee39a-af57-57fb-85af-9078c27cfcaf', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/478ee39a-af57-57fb-85af-9078c27cfcaf'}, {'abstract': 'The exhibit will include rare examples of highly prized shoes and more than a century’s worth of footwear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/arts/design/the-rise-of-sneaker-culture-tracks-coolness-at-the-brooklyn-museum.html', 'snippet': 'The exhibit will include rare examples of highly prized shoes and more than a century’s worth of footwear.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Don’t ever be caught out there with a pair of grips that everybody and their mother has,” Bobbito Garcia, high priest of sneaker culture, counseled in an influential essay for The Source magazine in 1991. What Mr. Garcia meant, translated, was: The harder a pair is to find, the cooler it is. Judging by that metric, the Brooklyn Museum should be the coolest place on the planet for sneakerheads with “The Rise of Sneaker Culture,” which opens July 10 and runs through Oct. 4. The show will include not only rare examples of prized shoes, like the original Nike Air Force 1 (a pristine 1983 pair can command $1,500), but also more than a century’s worth of footwear that united the casual, the athletic and the artful in a way that has become ubiquitous worldwide. One of the oldest existing running shoes, from the 1860s, will be featured, along with 1920s Keds. But the show’s main attraction will be examples of the shoes that meshed with hip-hop, the graffiti scene and basketball subculture beginning in the 1970s to lay what Mr. Garcia calls “the foundation for all the madness.” (718-638-5000, brooklynmuseum.org.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/arts/05GUIDE3_COMBO/05GUIDE2_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/arts/05GUIDE3_COMBO/05GUIDE2_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': No