e': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, Jeb', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Trump, Donald J', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Bridge 21st Century', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T11:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By First Draft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'First', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Draft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/097608cb-707f-5058-a266-8e1b9dd6da84', 'word_count': 1604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/097608cb-707f-5058-a266-8e1b9dd6da84'}, {'abstract': 'The only way I could get through to my nephew, suddenly a quadriplegic, was with the dark, dirgy music.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/02/understanding-the-incomprehensible-with-music/', 'snippet': 'The only way I could get through to my nephew, suddenly a quadriplegic, was with the dark, dirgy music.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I arrived home from the Shepherd Center in Atlanta — a “catastrophic care hospital” — debilitated by grief. I had many questions stemming from our family’s new reality, but only one that I would work to answer: What should I include on a soundtrack for recovery for my formerly active and able-bodied 23-year-old nephew who had just become a quadriplegic? I sifted through my iTunes and the pile of neglected CDs shoved to the back of my closet with a clarity of purpose and swollen eyes. There wasn’t much else I could do.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 592, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 592}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1011, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2022, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02private/02private-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Understanding the Incomprehensible With Music', 'kicker': 'Private Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T11:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeanann Pannasch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeanann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pannasch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdb2ac90-febb-5d2c-a51d-7d199bf06f25', 'word_count': 1320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdb2ac90-febb-5d2c-a51d-7d199bf06f25'}, {'abstract': '“White” is a cultural signifier that is closely linked to rationalism and colonialism — and white philosophers cannot ignore it.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/02/looking-white-in-the-face/', 'snippet': '“White” is a cultural signifier that is closely linked to rationalism and colonialism — and white philosophers cannot ignore it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the 13th in a series of interviews with philosophers on race that I am conducting for The Stone. This week’s conversation is with John D. Caputo, who is the Thomas J. Watson professor of religion emeritus at Syracuse University and David R. Cook professor of philosophy emeritus at Villanova University. He is the author of numerous books, including “The Insistence of God: A Theology of Perhaps” and “Truth.” His next book, “Hoping Against Hope,” will appear this fall. — George Yancy', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/opinion/02stoneWeb/02stoneWeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo