Sale', 'lead_paragraph': 'MIXED MESSAGES AND PARALYSIS IN GREEK CRISIS | In a day of twists and turns, Greeks could be forgiven for wondering who was in charge,  Andrew Higgins writes in The New York Times. On Wednesday, Prime Minister Alexis Tsipras of Greece said he would be willing to accept many of the bailout terms set by Greece’s creditors, only to go on television in the afternoon to urge Greeks to vote no on Sunday’s referendum on the bailout,  Suzanne Daley and Kiki Kitsantonis write in The New York Times. Then there’s the question of what Greeks would be voting on: The referendum pertains to the terms offered by creditors last week, an offer that in effect expired with the existing bailout package on Tuesday night. On Thursday, Yanis Varoufakis, Greece’s finance minister,  told Bloomberg Television that he would resign if Greek voters accepted the bailout. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Mixed Messages and Paralysis in Greek Crisis', 'kicker': 'News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Antitrust Laws and Competition Issues', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Home Appliances', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'val