n.html', 'snippet': 'Facing cancer, this novel’s heroine considers life, death and a long-ago nearly fatal voyage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If every stage of growth proceeds from a sort of metaphorical death, how many times in life does one person die? We cycle through our grand repertory of possible selves, striving in adolescence and adulthood for reinvention, yet our most enduring feature never recedes: We are always dying.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 628, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 628}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 979, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1957, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05STERN/05STERN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Life #6,’ by Diana Wagman', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Sea Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, Amanda', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wagman, Diana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:42:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Stern', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stern', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/58d4929b-1376-5dde-8b53-b25f2b468161', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58d4929b-1376-5dde-8b53-b25f2b468161'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist profiles the developers and devotees who have aided the rise of cybercurrency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/nathaniel-poppers-digital-gold-looks-at-bitcoin.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist profiles the developers and devotees who have aided the rise of cybercurrency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you wanted to create an entirely new world, like that in “Star Wars,” or in Neal Stephenson’s “Cryptonomicon,” you would certainly need an entirely new form of money. As Nathaniel Popper points out in his new book, “Digital Gold,” which tracks the roller-coaster ride of the digital currency called Bitcoin, it’s not surprising that part of its appeal to its devotees is that it could enable the creation of that new world, a libertarian utopia. Bitcoins — which are created, or mined, via complex computer algorithms, and are stored in digital wallets — offer privacy: Although every transaction is recorded, the users are identified only by wallet IDs, which are difficult, if not impossible, to track back to their owners. Theoretically, Bitcoins also allow users to avoid any fees or meddling by a middleman like a bank, because buyers and sellers can deal directly with one another. At its grandest, Bitcoin promises freedom from the clunkiness of cash, from the dominance of central banks and from technology that allows us to be tracked, even by our own governments. On a more prosaic level, why shouldn’t money be as vulnerable to technological disruption as, say, music?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 617, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 617}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 995, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1990, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05MACLEAN/05-Maclean-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nathaniel Popper’s ‘Digital Gold’ Looks at Bitcoin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coins of the Realm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McLean, Bethany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Popper, Nathaniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bitcoin (Currency)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:42:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bethany McLean', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bethany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McLean', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8f13713-de14-5cf0-8ac2-e284d3a43390', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8f13713-de14-5cf0-8ac2-e284d3a43390'}, {'abstract': 'A novel follows the Grand Tour of a landscape-design pioneer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/a-lady-of-good-family-by-jeanne-mackin.html', 'snippet': 'A novel follows the Grand Tour of a landscape-design pioneer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toward the beginning of “The \xadBeautiful American,” Jeanne Mackin’s absorbing 2014 novel of American expats in France from the 1920s through World War II, her heroine asks, “Do they matter, simple facts?” She could be summing up the dilemma of the historical novelist: How closely must fiction inspired by real people and events — as Mackin’s novels have been — hew to the bones of the \xadhistorical record? How much can the writer invent without betraying the reader’s trust? And, conversely, how much must the writer invent in order to transform history, in all its messiness, into fiction with a satisfying narrative arc and fully developed characters?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 834, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 834}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1473, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05RAKOFF/05RAKOFF-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Lady of Good Family,’ by Jeanne Mackin', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'High Cultivation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mackin, Jeanne (1948- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:43:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna Rakoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rakoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b89ac6d-5542-5643-b960-36f82937e6bc', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b89ac6d-5542-5643-b960-36f82937e6bc'}, {'abstract': 'A woman’s harrowing girlhood, as told to her analyst.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/the-body-where-i-was-born-by-guadalupe-nettel.html', 'snippet': 'A woman’s harrowing girlhood, as told to her analyst.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The girl at the heart of Guadalupe Nettel’s novel has a peculiar problem. She is a cockroach. At least her mother refers to her this way, as when she castigates her posture: “Cucaracha!” she yells. “Stand up straight!” Adding insult to insecthood, a birthmark on the child’s right cornea limits her vision. In order to strengthen the extraocular muscles, she must wear a flesh-hued cloth over her good eye. She resists putting it on each morning, but once it is secured, she submits to being “a patched child” until 5 o’clock, when she is allowed to remove it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 934, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 934}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 658, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1049, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05-Rowland/05-Rowland-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Body Where I Was Born,’ by Guadalupe Nettel', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s Bugging Me', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rowland, Amy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nettel, Guadalupe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lichtenstein, J T (Translator)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:43:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Rowland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rowland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/435bfacc-c947-54bd-8b85-8ed1f92b99dc', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/435bfacc-c947-54bd-8b85-8ed1f92b99dc'}, {'abstract': 'Joseph Luzzi turned to Dante to come to terms with grief after his wife’s death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/joseph-luzzis-in-a-dark-wood.html', 'snippet': 'Joseph Luzzi turned to Dante to come to terms with grief after his wife’s death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beatrice Portinari died at age 24 on June 8, 1290, and dozens of other figures in the “Commedia” were dead by the week in 1300 when Dante, poet and protagonist, crossed over to the other side. “The Divine Comedy” is a long poem about a lot of dead people, and Dante, no halfway believer in the hereafter, used his gifts — for speech, for the strong image, for extravagant simile — to bring them to life in words.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05ELIE/05ELIE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Joseph Luzzi’s ‘In a Dark Wood’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Path From Purgatory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elie, Paul', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luzzi, Joseph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dante Alighieri (1265-1321)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:43:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Elie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/78e5a461-292e-5b9f-a02b-8b703d9ce66f', 'word_count': 1081, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78e5a461-292e-5b9f-a02b-8b703d9ce66f'}, {'abstract': 'An Israeli writer describes a parent’s daily life in a combat zone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/etgar-kerets-the-seven-good-years-a-memoir.html', 'snippet': 'An Israeli writer describes a parent’s daily life in a combat zone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Etgar Keret’s story collections are like good punk rock records: fast, fun and cultishly admired. And yet despite the inclusion of a few foundation-rattling standouts, even the best punk records ultimately leave the listener under the spell of undiminishing sameness. The same goes for Keret’s collections. Not that Keret is completely to blame; his genre is the parable, and if it weren’t for the demands of the market, these stories would be individually packaged rather than bound in books. The best of Keret’s stories, “Surprise Egg,” say, from his 2006 collection “The Nimrod Flipout,” explode with distortion. In the case of that particular story, there’s also an actual explosion — a suicide bomb — and the story’s power lies in the immediacy of its ending: a bomb-\xadwidowed husband dreams of the foot of his dead wife slowly stroking his face. Keret forgoes easy moralizing and refuses to politicize the personal or to offer an obvious message. When Keret, who is Israeli and writes in Hebrew, first appeared in English, he was hailed as a savior of short stories from the dour realism of Raymond Carver, and a liberator of Israeli fiction from the big-picture density of writers like David Grossman. His stories were unapologetically absurd and libidinous, and if they weren’t all winners, then at least there was something refreshing in Keret’s imaginative conceits.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 801}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1123, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05WILSON/05WILSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Etgar Keret’s ‘The Seven Good Years: A Memoir’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Love and Terrorism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keret, Etgar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:43:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/63e7ea1d-35f4-51fb-9dc1-8d05052d560d', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63e7ea1d-35f4-51fb-9dc1-8d05052d560d'}, {'abstract': 'The Jazz Age figure Henrietta Bingham charmed the British.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/irrepressible-the-jazz-age-life-of-henrietta-bingham-by-emily-bingham.html', 'snippet': 'The Jazz Age figure Henrietta Bingham charmed the British.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to resist a biography with a preface that features the discovery of attic trunks filled with nearly 200 beribboned love letters, a first edition of Virginia Woolf’s “The Years” and a court ensemble once owned by the 1930s shorts-wearing rebel tennis star Helen Jacobs.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 736}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 835, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2000, 'width': 1630, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05subPURVES/05subPURVES-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Irrepressible: The Jazz Age Life of Henrietta Bingham,’ by Emily Bingham', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American in Bloomsbury', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bingham, Emily Simms (1965- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bingham, Henrietta Worth (1901-68)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T13:44:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Miranda Purves', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Miranda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Purves', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1ed3112-2079-5470-a1c7-030a9241a3bc', 'word_count': 674, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1ed3112-2079-5470-a1c7-030a9241a3bc'}, {'abstract': 'The American Revolution was also fought on the Gulf Coast.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/books/review/independence-lost-by-kathleen-duval.html', 'snippet': 'The American Revolution was also fought on the Gulf Coast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paint yourself a mental picture of the American War of Independence. If all you see are British redcoats battling minutemen and Continentals, Kathleen DuVal’s “Independence Lost” will knock your socks off.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05HOLTON/05HOLTON-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 422, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/books/review/05HOLTON/05HOLTON-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 422}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty