14:20:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/a85f64b3-e6c5-5862-9944-c5791cd668f1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/a85f64b3-e6c5-5862-9944-c5791cd668f1'}, {'abstract': 'Holly Madison, the former Playboy Playmate whose tell-all memoir is No. 2 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has said, “I don’t want negative, toxic people in my life anymore.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Holly Madison, the former Playboy Playmate whose tell-all memoir is No. 2 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has said, “I don’t want negative, toxic people in my life anymore.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here Bunny, Bunny: Holly Madison, the former Playboy Playmate and star of the E! reality show “The Girls Next Door,” about life at the Playboy Mansion, hits the hardcover nonfiction list this week with a tell-all memoir, “Down the Rabbit Hole,” new at No. 2. The book paints a fairly tawdry picture of Madison’s years as Hugh Hefner’s girlfriend — or, more accurately, as the “No.\u202f1 girlfriend” in a volatile harem rife with infighting, sexual competition and petty jealousies over money and favors. (I told you it was tawdry.) Hef has accused Madison of “rewriting history,” a claim she shrugged off in a recent interview with The Associated Press: “He doesn’t have any mental or emotional power over me anymore,” she said. “He’s somebody that I look back on as somebody who treated me really poorly, who I tried to convince myself was a great person but I don’t think is. And I don’t want negative, toxic people in my life anymore.” All of this is a good excuse, if you needed one, to dig up a copy of Gloria Steinem’s 1963 exposé of the Playboy Club in New York City, where she briefly assumed a false name and went undercover in the standard satin costume, complete with ears and a rabbit tail. “A middle-\xadaged man in a private guard’s uniform grinned and beckoned,” Steinem wrote. “\u200a‘Here bunny, bunny, bunny,’ he said, and jerked his thumb toward the glass door on the left. ‘Interviews downstairs in the Playmate Bar.’\u200a” Madison might recognize the world Steinem was writing about, even if it had less silicone than she’s used to; evidently, the bunnies of Steinem’s era padded their bosoms with plastic dry-cleaning bags.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12insidelist-image/12insidelist-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12insidelist-image/12insidelist-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12insidelist-image/12insidelist-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12insidelist-image/12insidelist-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12insidelist-image/12insidelist-image-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T14:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47b56d99-d687-53c7-aacd-43e36ed50b45', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47b56d99-d687-53c7-aacd-43e36ed50b45'}, {'abstract': '“UnReal,” a Lifetime drama about a reality dating show, recycles some painfully dark moments that its creator experienced as a producer on “The Bachelor.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/arts/television/lifetimes-unreal-recycles-a-producers-dark-experiences.html', 'snippet': '“UnReal,” a Lifetime drama about a reality dating show, recycles some painfully dark moments that its creator experienced as a producer on “The Bachelor.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'After six seasons of working as a producer on “The Bachelor,” Sarah Gertrude Shapiro was facing a breakdown. “I wasn’t thinking suicide, but I was close,” she said. “I wanted to burn my whole life down.” Ms. Shapiro, an avowed feminist, had somehow become an expert at manipulating female contestants behind the scenes to get “good television” for the reality dating show.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28UNREAL1/28UNREAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/06/28/arts/28UNREAL1/28UNREAL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im