news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gardiner Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gardiner', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ee40d10-0964-5377-b91a-4e94e4d5d181', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ee40d10-0964-5377-b91a-4e94e4d5d181'}, {'abstract': 'The Dominican Republic hasn’t yet acted on its plans to expel hundreds of thousands of people of Haitian descent. But the worst may be yet to come.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/02/magazine/the-dominican-time-bomb.html', 'snippet': 'The Dominican Republic hasn’t yet acted on its plans to expel hundreds of thousands of people of Haitian descent. But the worst may be yet to come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In early 2006, my first long-term overseas posting as a journalist took me to the Dominican Republic. From my new home in Santo Domingo, I planned to write about tourism, baseball, corruption and drug trafficking, while working on my Spanish. If things went well, I figured, I might even get to cross the island of Hispaniola’s international border, into Haiti, whose chronic crises — including a recent coup d’état that had overthrown the president — drew more international interest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/02/magazine/02mag-dominican-1/02mag-dominican-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dominican Time Bomb', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Migrant Labor (Agriculture)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Medina, Danilo', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dominican Republic', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haiti', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T21:19:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan M. Katz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6510c94c-27b1-5dd5-afe4-6bb4234457c7', 'word_count': 1932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6510c94c-27b1-5dd5-afe4-6bb4234457c7'}, {'abstract': 'Critics of biotechnology have long said that the current regulatory system is too lax.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/business/white-house-orders-review-of-biotechnology-regulations.html', 'snippet': 'Critics of biotechnology have long said that the current regulatory system is too lax.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Obama administration said on Thursday that it would update the way the government regulated genetically modified crops and some other biotechnology products, saying that the nearly 30-year-old system had become outdated and confusing and did not foster public confidence.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'White House Orders Review of Rules for Genetically Modified Crops', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Administration Orders Review of Rules for Genetically Modified Crops', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollack, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetic Engineering', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AGRICULTURE DEPARTMENT', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T21:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1faca745-3b93-5bf6-9f02-155e3b17f0f0', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1faca745-3b93-5bf6-9f02-155e3b17f0f0'}, {'abstract': 'The artist’s new work unleashes a firestorm of patterns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/arts/design/review-ruth-root-minimal-and-opulent-at-andrew-kreps-gallery.html', 'snippet': 'The artist’s new work unleashes a firestorm of patterns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a gesture of extraordinary generosity and transparency, the artist Ruth Root once made a news release with dozens of thumbnail images of phenomena that had inspired her work: everything from paintings by Peter Feeley, Mary Heilmann, Ellsworth Kelly and Josef Albers to shaped bathmats, ghosts and multicolored ski socks. From the looks of her current show, Ms. Root has stayed close to these origins — yet also strayed very far away.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 875, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 875}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 703, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1405, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/0703GALLERY6/0703GALLERY6-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Ruth Root, Minimal and Opulent, at Andrew Kreps Gallery', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ruth Root', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kreps, Andrew, Gallery', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Root, Ruth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T21:20:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbfd5545-afc4-5735-8dbb-966fdc08b3ea', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbfd5545-afc4-5735-8dbb-966fdc08b3ea'}, {'abstract': 'This show at Bridget Donahue is part archive, part reliquary and part toolbox.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/arts/design/review-susan-cianciolo-lets-others-pick-through-fragments-of-her-life.html', 'snippet': 'This show at Bridget Donahue is part archive, part reliquary and part toolbox.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The kind of visual and tactile magic that makes you remember why you loved art in the first place — way back, as a kid — is hard to find. But it’s there in Susan Cianciolo’s enchanting solo exhibition, her first New York gallery show in over a decade.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Susan Cianciolo Lets Others Pick Through Fragments of Her Life', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Susan Cianciolo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cianciolo, Susan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T21:21:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Holland Cotter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Holland', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70d012b1-5fe2-5cd3-a267-4234f4299201', 'word_count': 345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70d012b1-5fe2-5cd3-a267-4234f4299201'}, {'abstract': 'This vast suite of photographs installed in the Museum of Modern Art’s atrium has no images of people but still evokes a sense of human struggle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/arts/design/review-zoe-leonards-analogue-shows-the-human-toll-of-globalization.html', 'snippet': 'This vast suite of photographs installed in the Museum of Modern Art’s atrium has no images of people but still evokes a sense of human struggle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zoe Leonard’s “Analogue,” a vast suite of photographs installed across three walls of the Museum of Modern Art’s atrium, is an affectingly plangent update of Social Realist photography. Produced between 1998 and 2009, its 412 images — 342 color, 70 black and white — show examples of low-end commerce from New York to Africa, indirectly but evocatively representing the human toll of corporate globalization. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03ANALOGUE/0703ANALOGUE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Zoe Leonard’s ‘Analogue’ Shows the Human Toll of Globalization', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '412 Glimpses of Corporate Globalization&#8217;s Toll ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leonard, Zoe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-02T21:23:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2523733-dfd1-5269-a181-3182536edfcb', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2523733-dfd1-5269-a181-3182536edfcb'}, {'abstract': 'What to watch if you plan to spend the holiday indoors with a screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/arts/television/forget-fireworks-a-couch-potatos-tv-lineup.html', 'snippet': 'What to watch if you plan to spend the holiday indoors with a screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Forget fireworks; the real purpose of holiday-week down time is to catch up on overlooked small-screen offerings. Yet that means different things to different people. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03TVFOURTHJP1/0703TVFOURTHJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03TVFOURTHJP1/0703TVFOURTHJP1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03TVFOURTHJP1/0703TVFOURTHJP1-jumbo.jpg', 'h