Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/2ec45b62-1f41-5317-ad7a-4c89e55ea223', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/2ec45b62-1f41-5317-ad7a-4c89e55ea223'}, {'abstract': 'This high-society portrait painter put something extra into the work when his sitters were his companions and peers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/arts/design/review-sargents-intimate-portraits-of-friends-at-the-metropolitan-museum.html', 'snippet': 'This high-society portrait painter put something extra into the work when his sitters were his companions and peers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite a career as a society portraitist, John Singer Sargent was, by many accounts, a shy man, given to halting speech or silence except among people he knew well and liked. He was not ever, though, a shy painter. Few artists in any era have had as extroverted a hand as his, and as keen an instinct for visual theater. And when his sitters were people he cared for, something extra came into the work, a relaxed recklessness of a kind that scintillates and sluices through the 90 paintings and drawings in “Sargent: Portraits of Artists and Friends” at the Metropolitan Museum of Art. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03SARGENT/03SARGENT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1236, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03SARGENT/03SARGENT-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1236}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03SARGENT/03SARGENT-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 498, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03SARGENT/03SARGENT-superJumbo-v2.