ubsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38a1cb3d-1e45-5f70-96a0-abbe072483ef', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38a1cb3d-1e45-5f70-96a0-abbe072483ef'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Twain’s set at Nassau Coliseum featured her many country hits, with nods to other genres.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/03/arts/music/review-shania-twain-in-concert-recalls-glory-days.html', 'snippet': 'Ms. Twain’s set at Nassau Coliseum featured her many country hits, with nods to other genres.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNIONDALE, N.Y. — Everything shone brightly when Shania Twain performed at Nassau Coliseum here on Wednesday night. After a long residency in Las Vegas, she is back on the road for her first tour in over a decade — one that she has said will be her last. She brought Las Vegas with her. She had blond hair, sequined minidresses, thigh-high boots, banks of lasers, tall video screens and bursts of fireworks. For “Up!,” the title song of her blockbuster 2002 album, she was literally back in the saddle, one that was mounted on a cherry picker to lift her up and closer to the audience.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03TWAIN/03TWAIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 431, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03TWAIN/03TWAIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 431}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/03/arts/03TWAIN/03TWAIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 736, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr