de-UY1W-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/05/nyregion/20150705ALBUMss-slide-UY1W/20150705ALBUMss-slide-UY1W-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/nyregion/20150705ALBUMss-slide-UY1W/20150705ALBUMss-slide-UY1W-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Hybrid of Nature and the Man-Made', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-03T06:55:59+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/755f96ce-44a7-5ff0-96d3-75a4d246fc6a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/755f96ce-44a7-5ff0-96d3-75a4d246fc6a'}, {'abstract': 'Wandering the neighborhood at night, the photographer Miska Draskoczy found life abounding: green plants pushing up through broken pavement, vines reclaiming graffiti-marked walls, trees throwing bucolic shade over drive-by garbage heaps.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/nyregion/a-photographer-finds-abundant-life-in-and-around-the-gowanus-canal.html', 'snippet': 'Wandering the neighborhood at night, the photographer Miska Draskoczy found life abounding: green plants pushing up through broken pavement, vines reclaiming graffiti-marked walls, trees throwing bucolic shade over drive-by garbage heaps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A tree grows in Brooklyn, a rose grows in Spanish Harlem, a developer follows with seven-figure condos — are there any forces in New York more eternally indestructible than nature and gentrification? The photographer Miska Draskoczy became fascinated with this cycle of destruction and rebirth after moving near the Gowanus Canal in 2008, two years before the canal was named a Superfund site, requiring $506 million to clean up. Wandering the neighborhood at night, when the streets emptied of people, he found life abounding: green plants pushing up through broken pavement, vines reclaiming graffiti-marked walls, trees throwing bucolic shade over drive-by garbage heaps. One night, pushing through dense foliage at the dead end of Huntington Street, he spied a snowy egret perched on a branch, staring out over a motionless canal.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Photographer Finds Abundant Life in and Around the Gowanus Canal', 'kicker': 'Album', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forces of Nature ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gowanus (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Public Library', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Canals', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-03T06:57:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizati