lide-031L-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where ‘Perfect Tranquillity Reigns’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-03T10:59:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/1f6ba90e-6303-5906-ba31-a9896e07104a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/1f6ba90e-6303-5906-ba31-a9896e07104a'}, {'abstract': 'A spate of galleries and working studios set up by islanders and transplants has highlighted a growing community of artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/travel/the-arts-find-a-home-in-the-upper-florida-keys.html', 'snippet': 'A spate of galleries and working studios set up by islanders and transplants has highlighted a growing community of artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than an hour’s drive south of Miami, Islamorada in the Upper Florida Keys has a history of reinventing itself. Practically annihilated by the 1935 Labor Day hurricane (still considered the strongest to reach landfall in the United States), this “Village of Islands” became known for quality sport fishing, attracting professional and amateur anglers from around the world.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/05/travel/05HEADS1/05HEADS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/05/travel/05HEADS1/05HEADS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201