], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a12bbf75-c13c-58cb-99c1-f240b7369489', 'word_count': 1061, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a12bbf75-c13c-58cb-99c1-f240b7369489'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition on Buddhist art in Kashmir that traveled from one museum to another raises the issue of omissions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/04/arts/design/collecting-paradise-at-the-rubin-a-curious-case-of-divergence.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition on Buddhist art in Kashmir that traveled from one museum to another raises the issue of omissions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When art exhibitions travel from one museum to another, as they often do, their main themes usually don’t change very much. A show might be tweaked by different institutions, but the core set of objects and ideas put together by the originating curators ordinarily remains the same. “Collecting Paradise: Buddhist Art of Kashmir and Its Legacies,” a show at the Rubin Museum of Art in Manhattan, is a curious case of divergence from that custom, and it’s one that hinges on some currently momentous issues for museums and their holdings. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/04/arts/04RUBINSUB/04RUBINSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 912, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/04/arts/04RUBINSUB/04RUBINSUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 912}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/04/arts/04RUBINSUB/04RUBINSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/04/arts/04RUBINSUB/04RUBINSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, '