is, sounds as if he urgently needs to tell you something.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first 90 seconds of Meek Mill’s 2012 debut album, “Dreams and Nightmares,” are placid — no drums, just piano and strings. He’s rapping crisply atop them, contemplative but not preachy. The build begins. The beat drops. He goes from speaking to shouting. The effect is that of a missile strike on a rural pond. It’s one of the great triumphant moments in recent pop, and it suggested that for every time Meek Mill was wielding his words like a billy club, there was a meditation just underneath.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1208, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06MEEK/06MEEK-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Meek Mill Is Loudly Reflective on His Second Album, ‘Dreams Worth More Than Money’', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Rapper Is at Once Pensive and Loud', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meek Mill (Rapper)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-05T21:58:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3d659c4-3e1e-591e-b4ae-c3443ceaa600', 'word_count': 814, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3d659c4-3e1e-591e-b4ae-c3443ceaa600'}, {'abstract': 'Why Lynn Lempel’s puzzles make me smile.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/05/pass-the-coleslaw/', 'snippet': 'Why Lynn Lempel’s puzzles make me smile.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY PUZZLE —The rule when solving a Lynn Lempel puzzle is that you must smile while you work it. You most likely won’t be able to help yourself, what with the smoothness and fun entries, anyway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pass the Coleslaw', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-05T22:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1854c9f3-b32b-5174-a2a9-548cd85ce354', 'word_count': 515, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1854c9f3-b32b-5174-a2a9-548cd85ce354'}, {'abstract': 'This dance commission by Wayne McGregor, Olafur Eliasson and Jamie xx at the Manchester International Festival in England has breathtaking visuals and lighting, but no emotional resonance accrues.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/06/arts/dance/review-tree-of-codes-flaunts-jaw-dropping-effects.html', 'snippet': 'This dance commission by Wayne McGregor, Olafur Eliasson and Jamie xx at the Manchester International Festival in England has breathtaking visuals and lighting, but no emotional resonance accrues.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANCHESTER, England — “Tree of Codes,” a new work by Wayne McGregor, Olafur Eliasson and Jamie xx, inspired by Jonathan Safran Foer’s book of the same name, is the Manchester International Festival’s first major dance commission. This work uses dancers from Mr. McGregor’s company and the Paris Opera Ballet, an unusual combination that, along with its illustrious collaborators, helped build excitement. At the premiere here on Friday night, audience members screamed and cheered as they gave “Tree of Codes” a standing ovation.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06CODES/06CODES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1319, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/06/arts/06CODES/06CODES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1319}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima