, 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Therapy and Rehabilitation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T07:21:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/852fc4fe-3802-59e7-82a6-fd7675b9a69c', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852fc4fe-3802-59e7-82a6-fd7675b9a69c'}, {'abstract': 'A Rwandan genocide survivor’s story reminds us that simple narratives of good and evil, tribulation and triumph rarely capture the way life really is.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/opinion/david-brooks-the-courage-of-small-things.html', 'snippet': 'A Rwandan genocide survivor’s story reminds us that simple narratives of good and evil, tribulation and triumph rarely capture the way life really is.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I thought I knew the basic life story of my friend Clemantine Wamariya. She was born in Rwanda 27 years ago. When she was 6 — though she didn’t understand it — the genocide began and her world started shrinking. Her father stopped going to work after dark. Her family ate dinner with the lights off.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-jumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-superJumbo-v5.jpg', 'height': 1852, 'width': 1891, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-thumbStandard-v5.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/brooks-circular/brooks-circular-thumbLarge-v7.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Courage of Small Things', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Courage of Small Things', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weil, Elizabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wamariya, Clemantine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'War Crimes, Genocide and Crimes Against Humanity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rwanda', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wamariya, Clemantine', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T07:21:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Brooks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brooks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f183751-1b1e-5536-b606-c81181efcf71', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f183751-1b1e-5536-b606-c81181efcf71'}, {'abstract': 'Can companies offer both low prices and good jobs? Yes, a management professor finds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/opinion/joe-nocera-the-good-jobs-strategy.html', 'snippet': 'Can companies offer both low prices and good jobs? Yes, a management professor finds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Aspen Ideas Festival — an annual summer gabfest that presents all sorts of interesting ideas, from the improbable to the important — one of the big themes this year was jobs. How will America close the skills gap? Where will the good middle-class jobs of the future come from? I heard pleas for infrastructure spending as a job strategy, and creating jobs by unleashing our energy resources. There were speakers who believed that innovation would bring good jobs, and speakers who feared that some of those innovations — in robotics, for instance — would destroy good jobs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-thumbLarge-v4.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Good Jobs Strategy', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Good Jobs Strategy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nocera, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ton, Zeynep', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Supermarkets and Grocery Stores', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Good Jobs Strategy: How the Smartest Companies Invest in Employees to Lower Costs and Boost Profits (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T07:21:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df7f27e-7eb0-5946-9b21-76567bd28f35', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df7f27e-7eb0-5946-9b21-76567bd28f35'}, {'abstract': 'Could a set of proposed habitat-protection laws destroy America’s stock of cod and other food fish?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/opinion/a-knockout-blow-for-american-fish-stocks.html', 'snippet': 'Could a set of proposed habitat-protection laws destroy America’s stock of cod and other food fish?', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOSTON — NEW ENGLAND has long been synonymous with cod fishing. Since the 1600s, cod have been central to the region’s development, even giving Massachusetts’ famous cape its name. The fish were once so abundant, according to a local anecdote, that you could almost walk across the ocean on their backs.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 179, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 179}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 612, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/07Brogan/07Brogan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Knockout Blow for American Fish Stocks', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For All the Fish in the Sea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Fishery Management Council', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New England States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cod (Fish)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T07:21:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By GIB BROGAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gib', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'BROGAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3df7ef0-f858-5bb0-95a1-790817160dcb', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3df7ef0-f858-5bb0-95a1-790817160dcb'}, {'abstract': 'Shabab fighters killed 14 people and wounded 11 in the town of Mandera, a government official said, the latest attack in the region by the Islamist group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/08/world/africa/shabab-attack-mandera-kenya.html', 'snippet': 'Shabab fighters killed 14 people and wounded 11 in the town of Mandera, a government official said, the latest attack in the region by the Islamist group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAIROBI, Kenya — Shabab militants killed 14 people and wounded 11 in the northeastern Kenyan town of Mandera on Tuesday, a government official said, the latest attack in the region by the Somali Islamist group.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Somali Militants Carry Out Deadly Attack in Northeast Kenya', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Somali Militants in Kenya Stage Deadly Grenade Attack ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': "Kushkush, Isma'il", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shabab', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mandera (Kenya)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T07:32:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': "By Isma'il Kushkush", 'person': [{'firstname': "Isma'il", 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kushkush', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6061d211-a3d5-5d7a-bc20-245bcf05d17c', 'word_count': 292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6061d211-a3d5-5d7a-bc20-245bcf05d17c'}, {'abstract': 'A historian depicts the concentration camps where many of the Holocaust’s millions died.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/books/review/kl-a-history-of-the-nazi-concentration-camps-by-nikolaus-wachsmann.html', 'snippet': 'A historian depicts the concentration camps where many of the Holocaust’s millions died.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of an estimated 2.3 million prisoners who entered the hell of the Nazi concentration camps, over 1.7 million were killed, whether gassed or worked to their skeletal ends. Many went straight to their deaths, including about 870,000 Jews murdered on arrival and without registration at Auschwitz; others endured a slower annihilation, reduced to what Hannah Arendt called “ghastly marionettes with human faces.” Millions more were killed in other ways, but there were also survivors, and at the end of “KL: A History of the Nazi Concentration Camps,” his monumental study of the SS camps, Nikolaus Wachsmann focuses on one. His name is Moritz Choinowski, a Polish-born Jew detained by the Gestapo in 1939 in the German town of Magdeburg. By the time of his liberation on April 29, 1945, Choinow\xadski has survived Buchenwald, Auschwitz, a slowly growing German camp called Gross-Rosen and finally Dachau, as well as the nightmarish forms of transportation between them. “Is this possible?” he sobs in the Dachau infirmary. It was, just.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2046, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12COHEN1/12COHEN1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘KL: A History of the Nazi Concentration Camps,’ by Nikolaus Wachsmann', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Cruelty and the Depravity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Roger (Per)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wachsmann, Nikolaus (Author)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Concentration Camps', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'KL: A History of the Nazi Concentration Camps (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roger Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a70aacf0-865d-53c0-b45f-09e4af65ebc3', 'word_count': 1607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a70aacf0-865d-53c0-b45f-09e4af65ebc3'}, {'abstract': 'A husband’s affair, cruelly revealed, leaves his family’s future uncertain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/books/review/among-the-ten-thousand-things-by-julia-pierpont.html', 'snippet': 'A husband’s affair, cruelly revealed, leaves his family’s future uncertain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In some cases, the key to the success of a longstanding marriage may not be in its well-kept secrets but in its tacit agreements. Many a promiscuous spouse has been allowed room to roam as long as his or her dalliances aren’t flaunted. I remember reading an interview with Magic Johnson’s wife, Cookie, in this very newspaper in May 1996, five years after he was told he had been infected with H.I.V. When she came to Los Angeles to visit, “he always respected me,” she said. “It’s not that I thought he was a saint. But I didn’t know what was happening, and I preferred it that way.” If a marriage is sufficiently loving (as the Johnsons’ apparently still is) or satisfying or economically advantageous, maybe it isn’t worth blowing up if the person being betrayed is given the opportunity to turn a blind eye.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12SCHULMAN/12-Schulman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 606, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12SCHULMAN/12-Schulman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 606}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12SCHULMAN/12-Schulman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1014, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12SCHULMAN/12-Schulman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1899, 'width': 1879, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12SCHULMAN/12-Schulman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thu