 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebabb4aa-9f06-5dc6-85c1-8c57ae200b0f', 'word_count': 1345, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebabb4aa-9f06-5dc6-85c1-8c57ae200b0f'}, {'abstract': 'Karl Lagerfeld invited guests like Julianne Moore and Kristen Stewart to play roulette in the middle of the show.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/paris-couture-chanel-casino/', 'snippet': 'Karl Lagerfeld invited guests like Julianne Moore and Kristen Stewart to play roulette in the middle of the show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve seen everything from a supermarket to a brasserie; and today, Karl Lagerfeld upped the ante by turning Chanel’s fall 2015 couture show into a giant, bustling casino. As guests entered Paris’s Grand Palais, they were greeted by the sight of Chanel-branded slot machines and roulette tables. When the show began, celebrities — including Julianne Moore, Kristen Stewart, Rita Ora, Lara Stone, Lily-Rose Depp and Vanessa Paradis — walked out in their best Chanel eveningwear, took a seat at the tables in the center of the room, and began to play roulette. Next, models including Binx Walton and Sasha Pivovarova came out in severe, Kabuki-like wigs with strong, dark eyebrows and bright red, matte lips. They wore an updated assortment of Chanel’s signature tweeds, long, lace dresses with white collars, and evening dresses with elaborate beading and feathers. And, since no Chanel show would be complete without one, the spectacle ended with a Couture bride: this time, it was Kendall Jenner, who took a trip around the room in a white suit with a long tulle veil. It was as if Bianca Jagger had spent a day in Vegas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Couture Slots! Chanel Spends a Day at the Casino', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture (Fashion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagerfeld, Karl', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T16:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malina Joseph Gilchrist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malina', 'middlename': 'Joseph', 'lastname': 'Gilchrist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5f2a1e4-8520-5c46-ac6f-cee51713264f', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5f2a1e4-8520-5c46-ac6f-cee51713264f'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Christie wants an apology from the media, and Mr. Trump is surprised that reaction to his anti-immigrant comments has been so “severe.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/donald-trump-chris-christie-and-the-republican-race-to-the-bottom/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Christie wants an apology from the media, and Mr. Trump is surprised that reaction to his anti-immigrant comments has been so “severe.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'While your attention is riveted on the competition for the Republican presidential nomination (well, maybe not riveted, or even focused, but maybe occasionally mildly interested), there is another contest among Republican politicians – to say the most obviously ridiculous thing that pops into their heads.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7rosenthalWEB/7rosenthalWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 465, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'lega