t load and small size mean it can easily expand. Look for more deals to come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The latest pharmaceutical industry acquisition attempt leaves lots of room to grow. Horizon Pharma’s $1.75 billion unsolicited offer for its American rival Depomed continues the sector’s deal-making trend, fed by tax savings and cost cuts. Horizon is a relatively recent arrival on the scene, though, and its low debt load and small size mean it could easily expand.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Horizon Pharma’s Bid for Depomed Leaves Room to Grow  ', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drugs (Pharmaceuticals)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Horizon Pharma Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Depomed Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T19:20:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Cyran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cyran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fbd4440-3f01-5809-9876-970411eb734b', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fbd4440-3f01-5809-9876-970411eb734b'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Buress, the stand-up comic, discusses his new Comedy Central show and what it’s like to prepare for a roast, among other things — just not Bill Cosby.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/08/arts/television/hannibal-buress-on-his-new-show-justin-bieber-and-hawaiian-shirts.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Buress, the stand-up comic, discusses his new Comedy Central show and what it’s like to prepare for a roast, among other things — just not Bill Cosby.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It might be hard to believe, but “Why? With Hannibal Buress,” which begins  Wednesday on Comedy Central, is that comic’s first solo TV show.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 678, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1356, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/08/arts/08HANNIBAL/08HANNIBAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hannibal Buress on His New Show, Justin Bieber and Hawaiian Shirts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Roasting Bieber, and Other Signs of a Surge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buress, Hannibal', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Comedy Central', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bieber, Justin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cosby, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Why? With Hannibal Buress (TV Program)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T19:29:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy Egner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Egner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/725d5b13-7fe0-573f-a2df-9e339c61a73c', 'word_count': 1480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/725d5b13-7fe0-573f-a2df-9e339c61a73c'}, {'abstract': 'The Vancouver-based luxury-basics line Kit & Ace is crossing the continent in an Airstream trailer called the Copper Studio.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/kit-ace-copper-studio/', 'snippet': 'The Vancouver-based luxury-basics line Kit & Ace is crossing the continent in an Airstream trailer called the Copper Studio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer, the Vancouver-based clothing line Kit & Ace is embarking on a road trip across America. Traveling in a beautifully designed Airstream trailer called the Copper Studio, the Canadian company, conceived in 2014 by J.J. and Shannon Wilson (the son and wife, respectively, of the Lululemon founder Chip Wilson), is aiming to make its mark on American soil. Kit & Ace rethinks “athleisure,” one of the most successful — and saturated — markets in fashion, with luxury tees made from machine-washable cashmere designed, in Shannon’s words, “for people living full-contact lives.” Throughout the spring and summer, the Copper Studio, which began its journey at Coachella in April, has been operating as a traveling Kit & Ace pop-up shop, stopping in 24 cities and inviting customers to browse or lounge on one of two custom Acapulco chairs by Innit Designs placed outside. The Copper Studio has now arrived on the East Coast, where it will make its final stops in New York, Boston, Philadelphia and the Hamptons this month.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/t-magazine/07kit-bobb-1/07kit-bobb-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Canadian Clothing Line Takes a Very American Road Trip', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Road Trips', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Airstream Division of Thor Industries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T19:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Bobb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bobb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3063cd8-bce1-52f2-95f4-92aa1da97aa3', 'word_count': 254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3063cd8-bce1-52f2-95f4-92aa1da97aa3'}, {'abstract': 'Tales of a disaster foretold, many decades ago.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/milton-friedman-irving-fisher-and-greece/', 'snippet': 'Tales of a disaster foretold, many decades ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I continue to be amazed by how many people regard debt relief and devaluation as wild-eyed radical ideas; of course, it matters most that so many influential people in Europe share this ignorance. Anyway, for the record (and for my own future reference) I thought it would be helpful to post what Milton Friedman and Irving Fisher had to say about the Greek disaster. OK, they weren’t writing specifically about Greece — Friedman was writing in 1950, Fisher in 1933. But their analyses ring truer than ever.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Milton Friedman, Irving Fisher, and Greece', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-07T19:32:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/146c66e1-0d77-5314-9748-9bb18badf702', 'word_count': 257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/146c66e1-0d77-5314-9748-9bb18badf702'}, {'abstract': 'Giving governments access to encrypted online communication systems would make those systems vulnerable to hacking, says a new report.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/07/why-a-back-door-to-the-internet-is-a-bad-idea/', 'snippet': 'Giving governments access to encrypted online communication systems would make those systems vulnerable to hacking, says a new report.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent months top American and British political leaders have been arguing that there should be no encrypted communication system that they cannot unlock whenever they deem it necessary to do so. Officials like the director of the National Security Agency, Michael Rogers, and Prime Minister David Cameron have said that unless technology companies grant them the technical equivalent of a back door to snoop on encrypted communications, the world’s bad guys will “go dark” and become untraceable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1340, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/07/opinion/7bajajWEB/7bajajWEB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why a Back Door to the Internet Is a Bad Idea', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cameron, David', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogers, Michael S', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Snowden, Edward J', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'val