a931-ce903a28aaf2'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/08/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '5:20 P.M. (TMC) PHILOMENA (2013) With the help of a former political journalist (Steve Coogan), an Irish Roman Catholic woman (Judi Dench, in an Oscar-nominated performance, above left, with Mr. Coogan) seeks the son who was taken from her 50 years earlier, when she became pregnant as a teenager and was forced to live in a convent. In this drama directed by Stephen Frears, their sleuthing takes them to Washington after they discover that the boy was adopted by an American couple and worked for the Reagan administration as an adult. “Ms. Dench’s Philomena Lee glows with the radiance of someone serene in her faith despite inhumane treatment by the church,” Stephen Holden wrote in The New York Times. Her dignified performance and the screenplay — adapted by Mr. Coogan and Jeff Pope from Martin Sixsmith’s 2009 nonfiction book, “The Lost Child of Philomena Lee” — “stabilize a volatile blend of ingredients,” Mr. Holden added.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-08T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/38b6ac91-c2cd-50c6-b5aa-f6943d3a2471', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 