000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Daley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Daley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a58604de-6606-5b91-ba0a-544998e12ba6', 'word_count': 1169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a58604de-6606-5b91-ba0a-544998e12ba6'}, {'abstract': 'Being charged no percent sounds like a great deal, but it may not be the best one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/09/your-money/auto-loans/the-siren-call-and-dangers-of-the-zero-percent-car-loan.html', 'snippet': 'Being charged no percent sounds like a great deal, but it may not be the best one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Car dealerships tend to offer zero-percent loans in the summer, and this season is no different. But while a no-interest loan may sound like a no-brainer, consumers should weigh whether it is really the best deal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Siren Call, and Dangers, of the Zero-Percent Car Loan', 'kicker': 'Your Money Adviser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Financing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-08T17:46:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Auto Loans', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00c47dc9-4998-59ca-90bc-1b7286cb9539', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00c47dc9-4998-59ca-90bc-1b7286cb9539'}, {'abstract': 'In his poignant one-person play about a missing gay teenager, James Lecesne plays a hard-boiled detective, an emphatic hair stylist and much more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/theater/the-absolute-brightness-of-leonard-pelkey-and-its-chameleon-actor-return.html', 'snippet': 'In his poignant one-person play about a missing gay teenager, James Lecesne plays a hard-boiled detective, an emphatic hair stylist and much more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Absolute Brightness of Leonard Pelkey,” James Lecesne portrays nearly a dozen characters, including a New Jersey detective, a sullen teenager, her brassy hairdresser mom and the British proprietor of a dance-and-drama school. They’ve all in some way come to know the unseen title character, a flamboyant gay teenager who has disappeared. Mr. Lecesne is both a virtuoso performer and a bighearted writer (he adapted the play from his young-adult novel); bravo to the producers who’ve brought back this emotionally stirring production, which had a brief engagement at Dixon Place earlier this year, for a longer run. (Westside Theater; westsidetheatre.com) ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/arts/12GUIDE4/12GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/arts/12GUIDE4/12GUIDE4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/arts/12GUIDE4/12GUIDE4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy'