 were part of a coordinated attack, but  Nathaniel Popper writes in DealBook that they are likely to revive a debate about how regulators can better safeguard the markets from technological glitches. A person who was on a call with other members of the trading industry told Mr. Popper that New York exchange employees expressed panic as they acknowledged they had “lost control of the system.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Morning Agenda: Alarm Over Computer Failures', 'kicker': 'News', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eurozone', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Stock Exchange', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Airlines', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-09T10:57:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4ebd3b5-c9a5-5796-b9da-3b432c0de51e', 'word_count': 1811, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4ebd3b5-c9a5-5796-b9da-3b432c0de51e'}, {'abstract': 'The filmmaker on the risks he took to confront the perpetrators of genocide.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/magazine/joshua-oppenheimer-wont-go-back-to-indonesia.html', 'snippet': 'The filmmaker on the risks he took to confront the perpetrators of genocide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your first film, ‘‘The Act of Killing,’’ was a portrait of the perpetrators of the 1965 Indonesian genocide, in which perhaps a million people suspected of being Communists were killed. In your new film, ‘‘The Look of Silence,’’ you focus on the murder of a single victim, Ramli Rukun. What made his killing stand out? Ramli’s murder had witnesses. Tens of thousands of others in that region were taken to rivers and killed, their bodies left to drift out to sea. Ramli’s body was dumped on an oil-palm plantation. For survivors, talking about Ramli was a way of insisting that the genocide had actually happened.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/magazine/12talk/12talk-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/magazine/12talk/12talk-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1200}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima