': 'nyt://article/1515300c-5010-5958-96a1-3c4f04cc35bf', 'word_count': 1150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1515300c-5010-5958-96a1-3c4f04cc35bf'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Williams plays a 60-year-old bank officer in a passionless marriage whose life is shaken up by the company of a male hustler.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/10/movies/review-robin-williams-as-a-hustler-hiring-husband-in-boulevard.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Williams plays a 60-year-old bank officer in a passionless marriage whose life is shaken up by the company of a male hustler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Boulevard,” billed as Robin Williams’s final on-screen performance, the twinkle in his eye had faded into a dead stare. The mischievous, manic clown, beckoning us to follow him to a comic three-ring circus, has shriveled to an unreadable automaton with cringing body language. This isn’t the first movie in which Mr. Williams played dark and shrouded. His character in “One Hour Photo” (2002) was a creepy smiling psychopath. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/09/arts/boulevard-image/boulevard-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/09/arts/boulevard-image/boulevard-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/09/arts/boulevard-image/boulevard-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 539, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/09/arts/boulevard-image/boulevard-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1078, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'su