Several galleries are mounting exhibitions touching on impermanent art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an era when art is toppling gold and joining high-end real estate as a preferred instrument for storing wealth, ephemeral works tend to flummox the system. A piece that burns bright for a few weeks or months and then exists only in memory, or photographs?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10INSIDEART1/10INSIDEART1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Art That’s Made Not to Last', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Made Not to Last', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-09T20:54:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f59ae88a-23f4-5eb1-bd46-53b31557fa29', 'word_count': 363, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f59ae88a-23f4-5eb1-bd46-53b31557fa29'}, {'abstract': 'Gary Carrion-Murayari and Alex Gartenfeld will share responsibility for the 2018 show.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/10/arts/design/new-museum-names-triennial-curators.html', 'snippet': 'Gary Carrion-Murayari and Alex Gartenfeld will share responsibility for the 2018 show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Museum’s triennial, which began in 2009 and wrapped up its third iteration in May, has established itself as a kind of energetically internationalist, youth-oriented answer to the Whitney Biennial — the triennial’s first show was made up of artists 33 or younger. For its next installation, the museum has chosen two curators (one of whom is over that age) who have become well known in the art world for covering a lot of ground in a short time. Gary Carrion-Murayari, 34, who has been a curator at the New Museum since 2010 and was co-curator of the Whitney Biennial that same year, will join Alex Gartenfeld, 28, the founding deputy director and chief curator at the Institute for Contemporary Art in Miami, to curate the show, which will open in 2018.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non