 was not an aesthetically coherent and compelling ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the center of the Ming dynasty play “The Peony Pavilion” is a love story that advances through dreams and visions and across the boundaries between life and death. That’s a feature it has in common with Romantic ballets. On this commonality rests the hopes of the National Ballet of China’s 2008 production of “Peony Pavilion,” which itself seeks to fulfill a dream: to create a Western ballet with Chinese characteristics.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINABALLET-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 352, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINABALLET-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 352}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINABALLET-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINABALLET-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1201, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINABALLET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINABALLET-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/arts/10CHINABALLET/10CHINA