.M. (13) HARPER LEE: AMERICAN MASTERS In honor of the release of Ms. Lee’s novel “Go Set a Watchman” on Tuesday, Mary McDonagh Murphy — who was able to read an advance copy — has updated her 2011 documentary, “Hey, Boo: Harper Lee and ‘To Kill a Mockingbird.’\xa0” (Above, Ms. Lee with Gregory Peck at the movie’s premiere in 1962.) Ms. Murphy will also post on Twitter (#HarperLeePBS) during the broadcast. “It gives the fascinating history of how the book came to be, which involved Truman Capote, a perceptive editor and an act of generosity that helped give Ms. Lee, the novel’s author, the time she needed to write,” Neil Genzlinger wrote in The New York Times of the original documentary, adding that the best moments come from other authors, who read passages and talk about the book’s impact on their work. “But just when this is beginning to seem like pandering,” he added, Oprah Winfrey “gives the most eloquent reading of all, of the scene in which Atticus leaves the courtroom after the pivotal verdict.” The broadcast is part of “Thirteen Days of Harper Lee,” an event featuring videos, book discussions and a link to “Essential Longform: The Best Harper Lee Reads,” assembled by the New York Public Library. (thirteen.org)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T05:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e919966-432c-5ef1-ade1-11b5fc982e66', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://art