'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Captain America: Civil War (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Young', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Young', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be6f2f16-8a68-5f79-b588-27797e23e87d', 'word_count': 3327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be6f2f16-8a68-5f79-b588-27797e23e87d'}, {'abstract': 'Domestic workers, who have long been denied equal protection in the work force, are now at the forefront of a growing and perhaps pivotal rights movement.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/10/organizing-for-the-right-to-care/', 'snippet': 'Domestic workers, who have long been denied equal protection in the work force, are now at the forefront of a growing and perhaps pivotal rights movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marlene Juarez worked as a nanny for a family near Boston, taking care of four children ranging in age from 6 months to 6 years old; she organized play dates, cooked, did laundry and cleaned a large house. Both parents worked full time and in some weeks asked Juarez to work as many as 60 or 70 hours. Juarez had recently emigrated from Honduras, and was afraid to complain. She couldn’t afford to lose her job. But, once, she requested a few hours off to deal with a personal matter — and in response, her employers docked her pay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-fixes/opinionator-pog-fixes-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/