: '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Fort’ and ‘The Secrets of Blueberries, Brothers, Moose & Me’', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Go Outside', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DeFelice, Cynthia (1951- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nickerson, Sara (1963- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fort (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Secrets of Blueberries, Brothers, Moose & Me (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:19:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Natalie Standiford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natalie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Standiford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/89f3c09d-21fb-513b-ac8b-93fe36fb2dc0', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89f3c09d-21fb-513b-ac8b-93fe36fb2dc0'}, {'abstract': 'A shapeshifter with a love of mischief comes to the aid of a supervillain out to expose an insincere hero.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/books/review/nimona-by-noelle-stevenson.html', 'snippet': 'A shapeshifter with a love of mischief comes to the aid of a supervillain out to expose an insincere hero.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an unnamed fantasy kingdom, the \xadvillain in residence is Ballister Blackheart, formerly a good knight in training, who lost his arm in an accident. Heroes don’t have mechanical prosthetics, so Ballister decides to do the logical thing and become a villain. And what villain doesn’t benefit from a sidekick? Unannounced, Nimona arrives, a helpful, motivated shapeshifting girl who wants nothing more than to see Ballister’s evil plans succeed.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12-Hicks-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12-Hicks-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 456}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12-Hicks-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 897, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12-Hicks-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 897, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12HICKS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12HICKS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/books/review/12HICKS/12HICKS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Nimona,’ by Noelle Stevenson', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawn That Way ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevenson, Noelle (1991- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Nimona (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:19:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Faith Erin Hicks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Faith', 'middlename': 'Erin', 'lastname': 'Hicks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b83fef78-867f-5000-8bb8-161f59accb2c', 'word_count': 598, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b83fef78-867f-5000-8bb8-161f59accb2c'}, {'abstract': 'In this Y.A. novel, a young muralist learns of her mystical family legacy when phantom forces threaten her community.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/books/review/shadowshaper-by-daniel-jose-older.html', 'snippet': 'In this Y.A. novel, a young muralist learns of her mystical family legacy when phantom forces threaten her community.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the best urban fantasy, the city is not just a backdrop, but functions as a character in its own right, offering up parallels between personal histories and histories of place. That is certainly true in Daniel José Older’s magnificent “Shadow\xadshaper,” which gives us a Brooklyn that is vital, authentic and under attack.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Shadowshaper,’ by Daniel José Older', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fear Grows in Brooklyn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Holly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Older, Daniel Jose', 'r