as relegated to a series of international Citigroup outposts. So when his identity was revealed, it surprised no one that his lucrative book contract was canceled.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 905}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1509, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/10/business/dealbook/dbpix-hell/dbpix-hell-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'John LeFevre’s Memoir of Banking Debauchery Elicits Mostly Yawns', 'kicker': 'Book Entry', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Memoir of Banking Debauchery That Elicits Mostly Yawns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LeFevre, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Straight to Hell: True Tales of Deviance, Debauchery and Billion-Dollar Deals (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:34:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan A. Knee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Knee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/678844f2-d40e-54b0-a9a7-74d33322828b', 'word_count': 974, 'uri': 'nyt://article/678844f2-d40e-54b0-a9a7-74d33322828b'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHISKEY TANGO FOXTROT, by David Shafer. (Mulholland/Little, Brown, $16.) Vivid, witty prose animates this thriller, which explores the uneasy links between government surveillance and personal privacy. Shafer’s characters confront a shadowy cabal of powerful media magnates seeking control of the world’s data.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:39:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joumana Khatib', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joumana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khatib', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/dfa8251a-2ba9-5c49-9aa6-36843b21e43f', 'word_count': 450, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dfa8251a-2ba9-5c49-9aa6-36843b21e43f'}, {'abstract': 'For a satisfying hot-weather supper, cook a pot of hominy stew, with pork, chayote squash, zucchini, tomatillo and fresh sweet corn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/15/dining/pozole-verde-for-a-fresh-summertime-meal.html', 'snippet': 'For a satisfying hot-weather supper, cook a pot of hominy stew, with pork, chayote squash, zucchini, tomatillo and fresh sweet corn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you happen to be in a Mexican town on a warm summer night, head for the spot where street vendors set up for the evening and let your nose guide you past the tacos and empanadas and tamales to the fragrant steaming pozole stand.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1458, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/dining/15KIT1/15KIT1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pozole Verde for a Fresh Summertime Meal', 'kicker': 'City Kitchen', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pozole Brings Mexico to You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanis, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mexican Food (Cuisine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:40:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd8aa8ff-7a72-50c3-87ec-a34c7bdb1564', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd8aa8ff-7a72-50c3-87ec-a34c7bdb1564'}, {'abstract': 'The top-ranked Djokovic defeated Richard Gasquet, 7-6 (2), 6-4, 6-4, to advance to his fourth Wimbledon final. He will face Federer, who beat Andy Murray, 7-5, 7-5, 6-4.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/sports/tennis/wimbledon-2015-novak-djokovic-roger-federer-andy-murray.html', 'snippet': 'The top-ranked Djokovic defeated Richard Gasquet, 7-6 (2), 6-4, 6-4, to advance to his fourth Wimbledon final. He will face Federer, who beat Andy Murray, 7-5, 7-5, 6-4.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WIMBLEDON, England — With each gorgeous passing shot and fist pump from Andy Murray, the noise and energy at Centre Court surged. Murray was engaged in a critical fight to stay alive in the second set of his match with Roger Federer, and he was producing the daring, line-kissing shots to do it.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/sports/11TENNISweb3/11TENNISweb3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wimbledon 2015: Novak Djokovic and Roger Federer Set Up Rematch in Final', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Murray Is in Fine Form, but a Surging Federer Comes Out on Top', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldstein, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Andy (1987- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gasquet, Richard', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wimbledon Tennis Tournament', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:40:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/76bf1533-eeac-5edb-9eba-47eb34e7bda5', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76bf1533-eeac-5edb-9eba-47eb34e7bda5'}, {'abstract': 'Demographics are transforming Southern society. Will the G.O.P. respond in time?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/will-demographics-transform-southern-politics.html', 'snippet': 'Demographics are transforming Southern society. Will the G.O.P. respond in time?', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNLIKE previous Alabama governors, Robert J. Bentley is not a fount of oratory. Nor is he a champion of “kinder and gentler.” His dyspeptic refusal to accept federal funds for Medicaid expansion, in a state where more than one million of 4.8 million residents depend on the program, betokens a stunning indifference to the Hippocratic oath. (The governor is a dermatologist.) Yet Mr. Bentley recently took a page from the Obama playbook and used a surprise executive order to remove four Confederate flags from the Alabama Capitol grounds, not far from the very spot where Jefferson Davis was sworn in as president of the Confederacy.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 779, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 779}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 789, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1578, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12subSOUTH-web/12subSOUTH-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dream World of the Southern Republicans', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dream World of the Southern Republicans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Raines, Howell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southern States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bentley, Robert J', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minorities', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:43:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howell Raines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howell', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Raines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/7def6d19-2572-5bd9-b352-bf739c5b1a62', 'word_count': 1548, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7def6d19-2572-5bd9-b352-bf739c5b1a62'}, {'abstract': '“The Little Paris Bookshop,” No. 16 on the hardcover fiction list, is the first international hit for the German novelist Nina George, who has written almost 30 books in 22 years.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': '“The Little Paris Bookshop,” No. 16 on the hardcover fiction list, is the first international hit for the German novelist Nina George, who has written almost 30 books in 22 years.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Best Regards: The German novelist Nina George is prolific: In the 22 years since she started her career at age 19, she has written almost 30 books, including mysteries, relationship guides and steamy romances. (“Americans get all finicky when it comes to the graphic nature of my erotic scenes,” she has said. “They prefer to leave a lot of it up to the imagination: more Barbie doll sexlessness, less Anaïs Nin eroticism.”) But with “The Little Paris Bookshop,” new at No. 16 on the hardcover fiction list, George has her first bona fide international hit, the tale of a lovelorn bookseller who peddles his wares from a barge on the Seine and sees his calling as one part matchmaker, one part witch doctor. “There are books that are suitable for a million people, others for only a hundred,” he explains. “There are even remedies — I mean books — that were written for one person only.” The novel’s success has introduced George, happily, to the world of international publishing, as she describes at some length on her website: “The captains and navigators in publishing are women! From agents to list managers, women negotiate with other women on everything from money and content to sales,” she writes. “The agents and publishers always sign their first names to their emails: Vanessa, Cecile, Mirjam. From New York to Paris, they write: ‘Best regards/Yours, Christine, Rowan, Anna, Hedda, Elise.’ They discuss money with the cold clarity of a glass of vodka. No dancing around the issue as is so often the case in Germany. What’s even more cathartic, though, is the praise I’ve received from Cecile, Rowan, Anna, Hedda and Christine. No praise for my work has ever been as profuse and sincere as the compliments I’ve received from these foreign publishers in Paris, New York, Rome, Amsterdam and Riga. First they bought my book, then they wrote me long letters explaining what they liked about it. I suspect the reluctance to compliment authors and their novels is a typically German phenomenon. What if it makes the author too expensive? Or if the accolades go to her head? Or if she becomes, somehow, too difficult to work with? Nonsense! Bring on the praise, my dear German book people! It is pure joy.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19george/19george-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19george/19george-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 453, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19george/19george-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19george/19george-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19george/19george-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-10T15:47:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b63da2da-be98-5132-a892-a43e0a175702', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b63da2da-be98-5132-a892-a43e0a175702'}, {'abstract': 'George W. Bush, in a moderated discussion with Bill Clinton in Texas, talked of his decision-making as president and also said he did not plan to be a campaign surrogate for his brother Jeb Bush in the 2016 campaign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/politics/first-draft/2015/07/10/george-w-bush-hopes-jeb-bush-and-hillary-clinton-elevate-the-discourse/', 'snippet': 'George W. Bush, in a moderated discussion with Bill Clinton in Texas, talked of his decision-making as president and also said he did not plan to be a campaign surrogate for his brother Jeb Bush in the 2016 campaign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his appearance in Texas with Bill Clinton on Thursday, George W. Bush called for an elevated political discourse in the 2016 presidential campaign, defended his difficult wartime decisions, and said he did not plan to campaign for his brother Jeb Bush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/09/us/politics/09firstdraft-george/09firstdraft-george-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/09/us/politics/09firstdraft-george/09firstdraft-george-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/09/us/politics/09firstdraft-g