531e-8b05-e17f5a139654', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3942aa67-536c-531e-8b05-e17f5a139654'}, {'abstract': 'The English singer-songwriter introduced “Blood,” a new album of songs that go beyond love ballads to issues of heritage and identity, at the Bowery Ballroom.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/arts/music/review-lianne-la-havas-sings-of-love-and-identity.html', 'snippet': 'The English singer-songwriter introduced “Blood,” a new album of songs that go beyond love ballads to issues of heritage and identity, at the Bowery Ballroom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the songs of Lianne La Havas intricate cross-currents are something to sail above. There are cross-currents of rhythm and harmony, of melancholy and bliss, of romance and self-determination, of playfulness and purpose. But what comes across first is the sheer grace of her voice. It can be a breathy purr, a lilting tease, a liquid confession or a torchy declaration. She often introduces a melody by landing firmly on each note, then returns to toy with it in sliding, fluttering phrases, not for vocal display but to convey a sense of freedom.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/arts/11LAHAVAS/11LAHAVAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/11/arts/11LAHAVAS/11LAHAVAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/arts/11LAHAVAS/11LAHAVAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/arts/11LAHAVAS/11LAHAVAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 20