e all sick\xa0this happened. We\xa0wish we could turn back time. From this vantage point, everything seems obvious.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T04:44:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/6adabf51-ccbe-5cdc-9465-9cac9fe6d21e', 'word_count': 64, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6adabf51-ccbe-5cdc-9465-9cac9fe6d21e'}, {'abstract': 'Lampard will not play his first game for New York City F.C. as planned on Sunday after he sustained a calf injury in practice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/sports/soccer/frank-lampard-sidelined-with-calf-injury.html', 'snippet': 'Lampard will not play his first game for New York City F.C. as planned on Sunday after he sustained a calf injury in practice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Frank Lampard will not play his first game for New York City F.C. as planned on Sunday after he sustained a calf injury in practice, the club announced. Lampard, 37, was expected to be back at full speed by next week. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Frank Lampard Sidelined With Calf Injury', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Soccer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lampard Sidelined With Calf Injury', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lampard, Frank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sports Injuries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Football Club (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T04:45:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Keh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/41fc54de-1e87-5910-8056-b6cfea973061', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41fc54de-1e87-5910-8056-b6cfea973061'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/arts/television/whats-on-tv-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (HBO) 7 DAYS IN HELL (2015) Andy Samberg, below, stars as Aaron Williams, the brother of Serena with the behavior of John McEnroe and the hair of Andre Agassi, and Kit Harington is Charles Poole, a British prodigy bred to play tennis, in this satire about the longest match in Wimbledon history and the people who overinflate the importance of athletic events. Ms. Williams, Chris Evert, Jim Lampley, Soledad O’Brien and Queen Elizabeth (or is it?) play themselves; Lena Dunham, Fred Armisen, Mary Steenburgen, Will Forte and Michael Sheen also find their way into the story, which features revealing men’s underwear, a prison orgy and performance-enhancing cocaine. “If raunchy isn’t your thing, skip ‘7 Days’ and watch the real Wimbledon,” Neil Genzlinger wrote in The New York Times. But “if you’ve always wanted to chase down the anonymous tennis tournament official who intones ‘Quiet, please’ before a serve and smack him or her upside the head for the crimes of affectedness and self-importance, ‘7 Days in Hell’ is made for you. Bonus points if you recognize the well-disguised Howie Mandel.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T05:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/c757eda5-1d26-535e-89a9-24a44cbee27c', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c757eda5-1d26-535e-89a9-24a44cbee27c'}, {'abstract': 'Pete Rose, who continues to seek redemption more than 25 years after his banishment from Major League Baseball, was honored by the Normal CornBelters, a minor league team in Illinois.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2015/07/12/sports/20150712rose.html', 'snippet': 'Pete Rose, who continues to seek redemption more than 25 years after his banishment from Major League Baseball, was honored by the Normal CornBelters, a minor league team in Illinois.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pete Rose, who continues to seek redemption more than 25 years after his banishment from Major League Baseball, was honored by the Normal CornBelters, a minor league team in Illinois.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/sports/20150712rose-slide-UBBH/20150712rose-slide-UBBH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/sports/20150712rose-slide-UBBH/20150712rose-slide-UBBH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type