rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a94ffef-376d-55d6-b67b-edaab01608b0', 'word_count': 1218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a94ffef-376d-55d6-b67b-edaab01608b0'}, {'abstract': 'The fascinating, unspoken, assumption here is that everybody really is what you see — superhero, villain, warrior.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/comic-con-is-all-about-the-eyes/', 'snippet': 'The fascinating, unspoken, assumption here is that everybody really is what you see — superhero, villain, warrior.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN DIEGO — The weird thing about Comic-Con—well, one of the weird things—is that you can’t really hear it. Walk the halls for an hour, and you’ll pick up nothing but the low, anonymous murmur of people who are mostly looking at, not listening to, each other.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comic-Con Is All About the Eyes', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic-Con (Conventions)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Brien, Conan", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'San Diego (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T14:41:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6622e02f-4d20-55d1-95c0-39016d800a27', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6622e02f-4d20-55d1-95c0-39016d800a27'}, {'abstract': 'Still imagining bums on welfare.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/jeb-and-the-nation-of-takers/', 'snippet': 'Still imagining bums on welfare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Maybe we were unfair to Mitt Romney; Jeb “people should work longer hours” Bush is making him look like a model of empathy for the less fortunate. All the obvious points apply: longer hours would mean more GDP (if and when the economy ever gets back to full employment), but not necessarily better lives, especially if the increase in GDP doesn’t trickle down.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jeb and the Nation of Takers', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T14:44:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd547607-ca1e-5ef5-b73e-d9547b21e1b8', 'word_count': 396, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd547607-ca1e-5ef5-b73e-d9547b21e1b8'}, {'abstract': 'Iran’s insistence on the lifting of a ban on shipments of conventional weapons in and out of the country has become an obstacle to an accord.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/world/middleeast/iran-nuclear-deal-still-faces-difficult-issues-kerry-says.html', 'snippet': 'Iran’s insistence on the lifting of a ban on shipments of conventional weapons in and out of the country has become an obstacle to an accord.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VIENNA — Secretary of State John Kerry said on Saturday that there were still “difficult issues to resolve” before a landmark nuclear accord with Iran could be completed.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran Nuclear Talks Still Face ‘Difficult Issues,’ Kerry Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Kerry Sees &#8216;Difficult Issues&#8217; in Iran Talks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T14:56:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael R. Gordon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Gordon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da1a0540-750f-50eb-b4ab-e7d491d50a39', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da1a0540-750f-50eb-b4ab-e7d491d50a39'}, {'abstract': 'Musing on poets with Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/variety-a-poetic-acrostic/', 'snippet': 'Musing on poets with Emily Cox and Henry Rathvon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VARIETY PUZZLE— What makes a poet unique? That is the question addressed in today’s acrostic.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Variety: A Poetic Acrostic', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T16:00:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Gaffney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gaffney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b17e9421-0b6b-584c-aaee-e15fccbd77f4', 'word_count': 165, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b17e9421-0b6b-584c-aaee-e15fccbd77f4'}, {'abstract': 'When a family commissions a work, they’re more interested in stories, lessons and values, rather than in sensation.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/11/writing-books-very-few-will-read/', 'snippet': 'When a family commissions a work, they’re more interested in stories, lessons and values, rather than in sensation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Authors \xadare often asked where they get their ideas. As a writer for hire, my answer owes something to the songwriter Sammy Cahn. Asked which came first, the music or the lyrics, he said: “The phone call.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-articleLarge.png', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-jumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/04/25/opinion/opinionator-pog-draft/opinionator-pog-draft-thumbStandard-v2.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/