ute, your interview is over. Exit stage right.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/03/16/opinion/Bronson-Brittany-circular/Bronson-Brittany-circular-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Money, Sex and Las Vegas Pool Parties', 'kicker': 'Contributing Op-Ed Writer', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sin City’s Dirty Pools', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Las Vegas (Nev)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bronson, Brittany', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Casinos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Swimming Pools', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Hazards and Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waiters and Waitresses', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Models (Professional)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T18:20:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brittany Bronson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brittany', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/60ef72e2-7d05-5be5-91bc-74e42144f990', 'word_count': 911, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60ef72e2-7d05-5be5-91bc-74e42144f990'}, {'abstract': 'Can Alzheimer’s ever have an upside?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/my-mother-lost-and-found.html', 'snippet': 'Can Alzheimer’s ever have an upside?', 'lead_paragraph': 'DENVER — “CAN you make sure it has lots of stuff?” my mom regularly asked the server at her favorite Italian restaurant. Apparently, the minestrone soup was mostly broth with just a couple of noodles, which was unacceptable. “Really scoop it up,” she instructed, adding her nervous, friendly laugh. With her salmon, she demanded all sauces on the side, and an additional plate of lemon slices. Sometimes she brought her own dessert from the place she liked down the street. Her fastidiousness made me want to shrink into the jar of Parmesan cheese, but what could I do? That was my mom.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12alzheimers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12alzheimers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12alzheimers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12alzheimers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1799, 'width': 1199, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12ALZHEIMERS-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12ALZHEIMERS-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12ALZHEIMERS/12ALZHEIMERS-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Mother, Lost and Found', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'My Mother, Lost and Found', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Alzheimer's Disease", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knopper, Steve', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-11T18:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Knopper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knopper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdeb14b9-ab4a-5b69-8366-6968bd4ee62a', 'word_count': 1685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdeb14b9-ab4a-5b69-8366-6968bd4ee62a'}, {'abstract': 'Students once learned more out of the classroom than in it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/diy-education-before-youtube.html', 'snippet': 'Students once learned more out of the classroom than in it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EACH summer, when school ends, education mostly stops short, too. But it hasn’t always been that way. For the striving youths of 19th-century America, learning was often a self-driven, year-round process. Devouring books by candlelight and debating issues by bonfire, the young men and women of the so-called “go-ahead generation” worked to educate themselves into a better life.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/12/opinion/sunday/12education/12education-articleLarge.jpg',