', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-12T16:10:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel L. Swarns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Swarns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e81b12cd-299d-556e-a497-7fad3e94cade', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e81b12cd-299d-556e-a497-7fad3e94cade'}, {'abstract': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: The British government postpones a plan to evacuate children abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://iht-retrospective.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/1940-childrens-departure-postponed-by-british/', 'snippet': 'From the International Herald Tribune archives: The British government postpones a plan to evacuate children abroad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The British government announced today [July 12] that its scheme to send thousands of Great Britain’s children to safety in the Dominions and the United States had been ‘‘postponed for the present.’’ The move came as a severe blow to the parents of at least 100,000 children already registered for transportation to new homes overseas. Although it was said authoritatively that there was ample shipping space to carry the children — and that the stumbling block was the lack of sufficient naval vessels for convoy duty — it was understood from other reliable quarters that the shipping problem was one by no means easily solved. In fact it was reported there are thousands of children whose parents want to send them abroad privately who are unable to get accommodations on British liners. — New York Herald Tribune, July 13, 1940', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '1940: Children’s Departure Postponed by British', 'kicker': 'IHT Retrospective', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-12T16:12:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By International Herald Tribune', 'person': [{'firstname': 'International', 'middlename': 'Herald', 'lastname': 'Tribune', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1e83af4-5665-57be-9c9a-6b6b624c872d', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1e83af4-5665-57be-9c9a-6b6b624c872d'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/12/arts/television/whats-on-tv-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '5 P.M. (Hallmark) PAW STAR GAME Whitey Furred, Mike Troutbreath, Derek Cheetah, Carlton Frisky, Jose Catseco and other all-stars sponsored by North Shore Animal League America and Last Hope Animal Rescue and Rehabilitation swat balls and catch line drives in this kitten baseball game. Beth Stern hosts this inaugural competition; the Yankees radio announcer John Sterling and the sports analyst Mary Carillo call the play-by-play; and celebrities including Ed Asner, Maria Menounos, Mario Lopez and Al Roker cheer on the teams.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On TV Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-12T16:16:21+0