ion': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/527dac84-7c2c-5b16-afd3-3c106bbdf4d6', 'word_count': 1317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/527dac84-7c2c-5b16-afd3-3c106bbdf4d6'}, {'abstract': 'Signs point to a close collaboration between Harper Lee and Tay Hohoff, the editor who worked with Ms. Lee as her novel went from a dark tale to a redemptive one.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/13/books/the-invisible-hand-behind-harper-lees-to-kill-a-mockingbird.html', 'snippet': 'Signs point to a close collaboration between Harper Lee and Tay Hohoff, the editor who worked with Ms. Lee as her novel went from a dark tale to a redemptive one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 1957, a 31-year-old aspiring novelist named Harper Lee — everyone called her Nelle — delivered the manuscript for “Go Set a Watchman” to her agent to send out to publishers, including the now-defunct J. B. Lippincott Company, which eventually bought it.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 831, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1661, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13HOHOFFJP/13HOHOFFJP-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Invisible Hand Behind Harper Lee’s ‘To Kill a Mockingbird’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Invisible Hand That Nurtured an Author and a Literary Classic ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Harper', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hohoff Torrey, Therese von', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Go Set a Watchman (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'To Kill a Mockingbird (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-12T21:52:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Mahler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mahler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95a3202f-78c1-58b1-bb67-da3ba469a3cd', 'word_count': 1616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95a3202f-78c1-58b1-bb67-da3ba469a3cd'}, {'abstract': 'This HBO movie is as whimsical a discussion of this condition as you’re likely to find.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/13/arts/television/review-my-depression-the-up-and-down-and-up-of-it-offers-hope-to-others.html', 'snippet': 'This HBO movie is as whimsical a discussion of this condition as you’re likely to find.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a theater artist, the versatile Elizabeth Swados often works on the fringes, writing, directing or providing music for pieces that bend forms and challenge audiences. But she is completely accessible in an animated film on Monday night on HBO. The subject is one she no doubt wants everyone to understand.\xa0The movie is “My Depression (The Up and Down and Up of It),” and it’s about her struggle with that condition. The film is adapted from her 2005 picture book — the screenplay is credited to Ms. Swados, David Wachtenheim and Robert Marianetti (the three also directed the film) — and it’s as charming and whimsical a discussion of depression as you’re likely to find. And as musical. Yes, the song “Suicide Mobile” (sung by Steve Buscemi) may be a little dark for some tastes, but, like the rest of this film, it’s honest and forthright as it talks about a condition often misunderstood and misrepresented.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 305}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 947, 'width': 1865, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/arts/13DEPRESSION/13DEPRESSION-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: ‘My Depression (The Up and Down and Up of It)’ Offers Hope to Others', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Lesson on Depression, and a Hug for Those Who Know It Too Well', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Genzlinger, Neil (Per)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swados, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Depression (Mental)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'My Depression (The Up and Down and Up of It) (TV Program)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-12T21:56:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3