pecialty Products Corporation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bray, Chad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Alent PLC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-13T08:57:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73649aa6-f86d-5529-97b3-9e3e68cd615c', 'word_count': 471, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73649aa6-f86d-5529-97b3-9e3e68cd615c'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Hiltzik says the birth of Big Science occurred one spring day in 1929.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/big-science-by-michael-hiltzik.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Hiltzik says the birth of Big Science occurred one spring day in 1929.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Modern scientific machinery is increasingly international and astronomically expensive. The Thirty Meter Telescope (T.M.T.) taking shape atop Mauna Kea in Hawaii costs $1.4 billion, will be 18 stories high and is under construction in partnership with Japan and other countries. ITER, a $21 billion, 24-story-high project to explore nuclear fusion, is being built in France by seven international partners, including the European Union, Russia, China and the United States. The Large Hadron Collider (L.H.C.), which produced the Higgs boson three years ago, cost $10 billion, is 17 miles in circumference and straddles two nations.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1255, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CREASE/19CREASE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Big Science,’ by Michael Hiltzik', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Big Science’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hiltzik, Michael A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Big Science: Ernest Lawrence and the Invention That Launched the Military-Industrial Complex (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-13T09:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert P. Crease', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Crease', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b8f1ed1-9106-5f32-be6c-dff28418766e', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b8f1ed1-9106-5f32-be6c-dff28418766e'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Moore’s new book tells the story of the meteorologists who decoded the skies with observation and measurement, and then helped society read the codes, too.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/the-weather-experiment-by-peter-moore.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Moore’s new book tells the story of the meteorologists who decoded the skies with observation and measurement, and then helped society read the codes, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the 25th of October in 1859, the steam clipper Royal Charter rounded the island of Anglesey off the coast of Wales on what was supposed to be the celebratory last evening of its two-month journey from Melbourne to Liverpool. Some 500 men, women and children were nearly home, many feeling blessed with fortunes worked from Australia’s Ballarat goldfields. Gold bullion and specie were crammed into pockets, hidden in money belts and locked up in the strongroom.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 640, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1280, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt/0719-BKS-Barnett-Crease-Holt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Weather Experiment,’ by Peter Moore', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Explain the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moore, Peter (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Weather Experiment: The Pioneers Who Sought to See the Future (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-13T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cynthia Barnett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cynthia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e755990-026e-5e03-a85e-07d47f69a53b', 'word_count': 1026, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e755990-026e-5e03-a85e-07d47f69a53b'}, {'abstract': 'The 17th-century English physician and philosopher Sir Thomas Browne was a prolific contributor of novel words to the English language, and an intellectual who had a good-humored skepticism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/in-search-of-sir-thomas-browne-by-hugh-aldersey-williams.html', 'snippet': 'The 17th-century English physician and philosopher Sir Thomas Browne was a prolific contributor of novel words to the English language, and an intellectual who had a good-humored skepticism.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The history of English prose can be seen as a dialectical struggle between two tendencies: plain versus grand. The plain style aims at ease and lucidity. It favors simply structured sentences, short words of Saxon origin and a conversational tone. It runs the risk of being flat. By contrast, the grand style — also called (by Cyril \xadConnolly) “mandarin” — aims at rhetorical luxuriance. It is characterized by rolling \xadperiods decked with balanced subordinate clauses, a polysyllabic Latinate vocabulary, elaborate rhythms, stately epithets, sumptuous metaphors, learned allusions and fanciful turns of phrase. It runs the risk of being ridiculous.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 480}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 819, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1638, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19HOLT/19HOLT-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘In Search of Sir Thomas Browne,’ by Hugh Aldersey-Williams', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘In Search of Sir Thomas Browne’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aldersey-Williams, Hugh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "In Search of Sir Thomas Browne: The Life and Afterlife of the Seventeenth Century's Most Inquiring Mind (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browne, Thomas (1605-82)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-13T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Holt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/999c02d7-5647-52c8-83f6-6bb0e16c351b', 'word_count': 1608, 'uri': 'nyt://article/999c02d7-5647-52c8-83f6-6bb0e16c351b'}, {'abstract': 'By moving into cities, adding amenities and offering social hours, extended-stay hotels are vying to attract younger clientele and be a haven for business travelers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/14/business/at-extended-stay-hotels-a-sense-of-home-or-a-party.html', 'snippet': 'By moving into cities, adding amenities and offering social hours, extended-stay hotels are vying to attract younger clientele and be a haven for business travelers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mike Jones spends about 100 days a year on the road in his work as an executive at a chemical company. Traveling from his home in North Carolina to as far as Malaysia and Thailand, he prefers extended-stay hotels.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/business/14itineraries-web/14itineraries-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/13/business/14itineraries-web/14itineraries-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/business/14itineraries-web/14itineraries-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/13/business/14itineraries-web/14itinera