 to reach all young people, including boys, much earlier than college.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 405}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 691, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1381, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/opinion/14tue3web/14tue3web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To Prevent Sexual Assault, Start Early', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Prevent Sexual Assault, Start Early', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-14T07:21:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9c57601-6676-5af3-b310-2bf371cf193d', 'word_count': 404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9c57601-6676-5af3-b310-2bf371cf193d'}, {'abstract': 'Although Jeb Bush’s recent remark is being treated as a gaffe, there is in fact a lively debate among economists on the issue of longer workweeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/15/upshot/should-americans-work-more-to-hit-4-percent-growth-we-would-have-to.html', 'snippet': 'Although Jeb Bush’s recent remark is being treated as a gaffe, there is in fact a lively debate among economists on the issue of longer workweeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeb Bush said last week that Americans would “need to work longer hours” if we’re going to meet his ambitious target of 4 percent real annual economic growth — nearly double the average growth rate the Congressional Budget Office expects in the future. Then he clarified his remarks to say he was talking specifically about the 6.6 million American workers with part-time jobs who say they would like to work full time.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/11/upshot/11UP-Jeb/11UP-Jeb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Americans Work More? To Hit 4 Percent Growth, We Would Have To', 'kicker': 'Wage Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do Americans Need to Work More? It’s Possible', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barro, Josh', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, Jeb', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Working Hours', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-14T07:30:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Barro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4077ab7f-913b-57b4-a6b1-ebc12e42740c', 'word_count': 1084, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4077ab7f-913b-57b4-a6b1-ebc12e42740c'}, {'abstract': 'The missed payment was overshadowed by the agreement among eurozone leaders that would provide more aid to Greece and keep it in the currency union.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/15/business/international/greek-debt-payment.html', 'snippet': 'The missed payment was overshadowed by the agreement among eurozone leaders that would provide more aid to Greece and keep it in the currency union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — Greece missed another payment to the International Monetary Fund late Monday, slipping further into the debt morass that has devastated the economy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greece Misses Another I.M.F. Payment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eurozone', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Central Bank', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-14T07:47:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'International Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Ewing', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ewing', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46c75d07-3fec-5b3c-a433-323da3a6d113', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46c75d07-3fec-5b3c-a433-323da3a6d113'}, {'abstract': 'A diplomat stationed in South Carolina helped keep Britain out of America’s Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/our-man-in-charleston-by-christopher-dickey.html', 'snippet': 'A diplomat stationed in South Carolina helped keep Britain out of America’s Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Great Britain abolished its slave trade in 1807, sending the Royal Navy to blockade the coast of Africa and intercept slavers in an effort to shut down the commerce altogether. In 1833, it did away with slavery itself in all its empire. And yet when war over slavery broke out in the United States in 1861, the British government remained neutral, even though it was led by Lord Palmerston — who, according to Christopher Dickey in “Our Man in Charleston,” believed himself to be “the leader of Christendom in its opposition to the slave trade.” This meant that Liverpool shipyards could sell heavily fortified “merchant” vessels to the Confederates (easily converted into war ships) and London investors could buy cotton bonds, which helped finance the secessionist war.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Our Man in Charleston,’ by Christopher Dickey', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Her Majesty’s Secret Servant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandin, Greg', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickey, Christopher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Our Man in Charleston: Britain's Secret Agent in the Civil War South (Book)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-14T09:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Grandin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grandin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa864f94-7359-50af-998c-348097f6879d', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa864f94-7359-50af-998c-348097f6879d'}, {'abstract': 'Readers have a chance to enjoy this culture-hopping novel by Fran Ross, being reissued 40 years after most of the world ignored it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/15/books/review-oreo-a-sandwich-cookie-of-a-feminist-comic-novel.html', 'snippet': 'Readers have a chance to enjoy this culture-hopping novel by Fran Ross, being reissued 40 years after most of the world ignored it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fran Ross’s first and only novel, “Oreo,” was published in 1974, four years after Toni Morrison’s “The Bluest Eye” and two years before Alex Haley’s “Roots.” It wasn’t reviewed in The New York Times; it was hardly reviewed anywhere.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/arts/bookross-hp/bookross-hp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/14/arts/bookross-hp/bookross-hp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/14/arts/bookross-hp/bookross-hp-jumbo.jpg', 'hei