, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-14T12:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Siddhartha Deb and Anna Holmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Siddhartha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Deb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67b36bae-e00d-50c1-b1aa-73656ca62d0e', 'word_count': 1433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67b36bae-e00d-50c1-b1aa-73656ca62d0e'}, {'abstract': 'Using the qualifier “just” to suggest a small number — without going to the trouble of revealing the actual figure — represents a laxity that we should strive to avoid.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/14/just-what-exactly/', 'snippet': 'Using the qualifier “just” to suggest a small number — without going to the trouble of revealing the actual figure — represents a laxity that we should strive to avoid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve inveighed before about weasel words that present some description as noteworthy without specifying what is actually noteworthy about it. My favorite (or least favorite) example is “in recent memory” — “the biggest sports upset in recent memory.” Whose memory? How recent? If we think back just a little further, will we remember a bigger one? The phrase really suggests something like, “Off the top of our heads we can’t think of a bigger one, but we don’t have time to check.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Just’ What, Exactly?', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-14T12:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_de