me play — show little of the exuberance common at gatherings in Japan and the United States. Mariya Kozhanova, a photographer, sought out to find out why.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/blogs/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK/20150715-lens-cosplay-slide-P0PK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Identity Through Cosplay in Russia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-15T23:07:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Blogs', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/555d78f8-c91c-55de-a2f9-3db2f0abfb98', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/555d78f8-c91c-55de-a2f9-3db2f0abfb98'}, {'abstract': 'Three deep-conditioning options to tame and hydrate hair without weighing down the strands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/16/fashion/beauty/hydrating-masks-for-thirsty-hair.html', 'snippet': 'Three deep-conditioning options to tame and hydrate hair without weighing down the strands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As with most of my beauty gripes, I blame age for the latest affront — hair as parched as a California farm. “As we age, the amount of oil we produce decreases in the skin and hair,” Kenneth Beer, a dermatologist in West Palm Beach, Fla., confirmed. “This makes the hair shaft more brittle.” Tack on visits to the saltwater pool at the McCarren Hotel in my neighborhood, and my hair was thirstier than a hangover. But hunting for hair masks proved bewildering. Some were so heavy I could have substituted mayonnaise for the same drizzled-oil effect. Others were so subtle that any daily conditioner would have done the job at half the price. But a select few impressed. Below, three deep-conditioning options that admirably tame and hydrate without weighing down strands. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/15/fashion/15WWORKS_COMBO/15WWORKS_COMBO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy':