e/1d93e76c-33c0-572d-b5a5-d35f41ed2547'}, {'abstract': 'Achieving pastel hair is time-consuming and toxic — but for many millennials and pop stars, so very worth it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/16/t-magazine/rainbow-hair-trend-beauty.html', 'snippet': 'Achieving pastel hair is time-consuming and toxic — but for many millennials and pop stars, so very worth it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To keep hair the color of peonies or larkspur requires constant care and commitment: dyeing, then re-dyeing, then re-dyeing, like Sisyphus as drawn by Lisa Frank. Pastels fade much faster than darker shades, sometimes in two or three washes. And yet the rage for hair that glistens like sugary cake-icing has never been more intense. Locks of celestial rose, aquamarine and lavender drifted down the fall 2015 runways. At the Gucci show last spring, the model Marjan Jonkman’s platinum mop was dip-dyed a cherry-blossom hue, and paired with a baroque floral suit. At Louis Vuitton, Fernanda Hin Lin Ly walked out in a huge fluffy white coat and tresses the color of bubble gum, looking like a severe interpretation of Princess Lolly of Candyland. The princesses of pop music—Katy Perry, Miley Cyrus, Rita Ora, Demi Lovato—have all experimented with neon lavender, sea-foam green and soft periwinkle. Perry might trace her love of the palette to the artist and confectioner Will Cotton, who depicted the singer lounging on a bed of pale pink cotton-candy clouds in the cover art for her 2010 album “Teenage Dream.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16beauty-color-syme/16beauty-color-syme-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16beauty-color-syme/16beauty-color-syme-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty