person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab3fdd21-13b2-54a2-aa3b-a2b5d95b2387', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab3fdd21-13b2-54a2-aa3b-a2b5d95b2387'}, {'abstract': 'The psychologist on what the Stanford prison experiment continues to reveal about human nature.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/magazine/philip-zimbardo-thinks-we-all-can-be-evil.html', 'snippet': 'The psychologist on what the Stanford prison experiment continues to reveal about human nature.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1971, you conducted a psychological experiment at Stanford. You split your 21 student-subjects into guards and inmates, with you as the superintendent, and had them run a prison out of a basement. You had to abandon the experiment after six days, because it spiraled violently out of control. Now, 44 years later, there is a movie about it. Do you like it? I’m delighted with it. It conveys for the first time to a general public what this kind of experiment is like. There is another movie called ‘‘Experimenter,’’ about Stanley Milgram’s research, which also premiered at Sundance, and the second half is confusing. At one point, Milgram walks out of his lab, and behind him is a huge elephant. I saw the director at Sundance, and I said, ‘‘Why did you have an elephant?’’ He said, ‘‘People like elephants.’’', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1202, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1202}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 512, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1350, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19talk/19mag-19talk-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Philip Zimbardo Thinks We All Can Be Evil', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Philip Zimbardo Thinks We All Can Be Evil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ronson, Jon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zimbardo, Philip G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Stanford Prison Experiment, The (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-16T11:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Jon Ronson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Jon', 'lastname': 'Ronson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/addc82a6-f361-5181-ad8a-5743198de394', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/addc82a6-f361-5181-ad8a-5743198de394'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from DreamHack, where 23,000 people spend a weekend plugged in together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/magazine/power-in-numbers.html', 'snippet': 'Scenes from DreamHack, where 23,000 people spend a weekend plugged in together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The best historical analogy for the ‘‘hackathon’’ might be the 18th-\xadcentury custom of the barn-\xadraising. Despite the technological abyss separating the two rituals, they are structurally akin: Each is a social observance in which a lot of people focus their collective will on an undertaking too great for any individual to carry out alone. Though the informal practice of hackathons arose slightly earlier, the first use of the portmanteau name dates to 1999, when Sun Microsystems and the freeware operating system OpenBSD each staged events under that banner to write very specialized pieces of software very quickly.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19hack1_main/19mag-19hack-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 463, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19hack1_main/19mag-19hack-t_CA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 463}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/magazine/19hack1_main/19mag-19hack-t_CA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '