urday with the usual range of lifestyle products, plus ample bouquets of fresh-cut blooms.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/16/flowerboy-project-sean-knibb/', 'snippet': 'Flowerboy Project opens Saturday with the usual range of lifestyle products, plus ample bouquets of fresh-cut blooms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sean Knibb has emerged as a key stakeholder in the Los Angeles design scene for his quirky, slightly surreal elevation of everyday materials, both indoors and out. Now, fans of his environmental design work — which includes, most famously, the industrial-chic Line hotel in Koreatown — can take home a piece of his sensibility at Knibb’s first retail space, Flowerboy Project, opening Saturday adjacent to the Knibb Design studio in Venice, Calif. Like many lifestyle stores in the neighborhood, the space includes a selection of design, fashion and beauty goods — but is anchored by a daily selection of fresh-cut blooms (bouquets come prearranged in jars in sizes small, medium and large).', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16flowerboy-beddie/16flowerboy-beddie-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A New L.A. Lifestyle Shop With a Bodega Sensibility', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Flower Hour', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-16T17:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eviana Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eviana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c02a3282-ae9d-5f1a-a189-01c8d0ee0118', 'word_count': 285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c02a3282-ae9d-5f1a-a189-01c8d0ee0118'}, {'abstract': 'Increasingly graphic representations of sexual violence in productions like “Guillaume Tell” and “The Abduction From the Seraglio” are coming in for questioning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/arts/music/when-cries-of-rape-are-heard-in-opera-halls.html', 'snippet': 'Increasingly graphic representations of sexual violence in productions like “Guillaume Tell” and “The Abduction From the Seraglio” are coming in for questioning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the beginning of “Don Giovanni,” a masked man breaks into a woman’s room in the middle of the night. Later in the opera, the woman — Donna Anna — describes the encounter to her fiancé as an attempted rape. Do we believe her? While we can sift through Mozart’s harmonies for proof, we always return to Anna’s words: The man “wanted to embrace me. As I tried to free myself, he held me even tighter.” ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 457, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 457}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 780, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1561, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELL1/19TELL1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Cries of Rape Are Heard in Opera Halls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Opera&#8217;s Troubled Tone: Scenes of Rape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Violence (Media and Entertainment)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Opera House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Guillaume Tell (Opera)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mozart, Wolfgang Amadeus', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Abduction From the Seraglio (Opera)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Don Giovanni (Opera)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-16T17:02:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micaela Baranello', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micaela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baranello', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2ca15f4-19e1-506b-b670-bc416024b25f', 'word_count': 1498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2ca15f4-19e1-506b-b670-bc416024b25f'}, {'abstract': 'Opera has its tough, demanding crowds that are quick to show displeasure with stars, directors and the operas themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/arts/music/the-boos-in-the-balcony-and-their-targets.html', 'snippet': 'Opera has its tough, demanding crowds that are quick to show displeasure with stars, directors and the operas themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The uproar at the Royal Opera over its new production of Rossini’s “Guillaume Tell” is part of a venerable operatic tradition. Such fury can surprise the uninitiated, who mistakenly think of opera as safe, effete entertainment — and not as the blood sport that aficionados know, love and, sometimes, love to hate. Booing, like opera, can be divided into several genres.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELL-SIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 987, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELL-SIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 987}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELL-SIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 623, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELL-SIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1246, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELLSIDE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELLSIDE1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/arts/19TELLSIDE1/19TELLSIDE1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thu