', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cotter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b0725de-031d-55bc-8c9a-be6cb331c777', 'word_count': 1480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b0725de-031d-55bc-8c9a-be6cb331c777'}, {'abstract': 'This opera, drawn from Greek myth, has lukewarm fans even among Strauss lovers, but this superb concert performance showed a composer at his most ingenious.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/17/arts/music/review-in-daphne-the-cleveland-orchestra-takes-on-a-difficult-strauss-opera.html', 'snippet': 'This opera, drawn from Greek myth, has lukewarm fans even among Strauss lovers, but this superb concert performance showed a composer at his most ingenious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though Richard Strauss thought highly of his one-act 1938 opera “Daphne,” a “bucolic tragedy,” as he and the librettist Joseph Gregor called it, posterity has not agreed. Even many Strauss-lovers feel that the score, while sumptuous and beguiling, is not top-tier Strauss, until the final scene of this mythological fantasy, when Daphne, a nature girl who longs to bond with the woods and meadows, is transformed by the gods into a tree. Here at last in this intricate, slightly strange music is Strauss at his most ingenious.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/17/arts/17DAPHNE/17DAPHNE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/17/arts/17DAPHNE/17DAPHNE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/17/arts/17DAPHNE/17DAPHNE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 604, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr