. (Bay Back/\xadLittle, Brown, $16.) A couple expecting their first child confront a feral, apocalyptic reality and future. After Cal and Frida are driven from a dystopic Los Angeles into the wilderness, they attempt to eke out a life of security.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joumana Khatib', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joumana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khatib', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/bb2b653a-4657-5c71-9d8c-e18202733deb', 'word_count': 472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bb2b653a-4657-5c71-9d8c-e18202733deb'}, {'abstract': 'A novelist argues that ‘The Pickwick Papers’ was hijacked from its illustrator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/death-and-mr-pickwick-by-stephen-jarvis.html', 'snippet': 'A novelist argues that ‘The Pickwick Papers’ was hijacked from its illustrator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You don’t normally wish a novel would come with a bibliography, but Stephen Jarvis’s new book practically begs for one. “Death and Mr. Pickwick” makes some eyebrow-raising claims: that Charles Dickens’s “The Pickwick Papers” was “so widely circulated that only the Bible, Shakespeare and perhaps the Book of Common Prayer can better its circulation”; that for a century or more Samuel Pickwick, the book’s genial bumbling hero, was “the most famous character in fiction there has ever been”; and that Dickens didn’t name or invent Samuel Pickwick.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Death and Mr. Pickwick,’ by Stephen Jarvis', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Dickens', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jarvis, Stephen (Author)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Death and Mr Pickwick (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Upchurch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Upchurch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d471a0ff-3d2f-5913-b2e5-1daf8eefa8db', 'word_count': 586, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d471a0ff-3d2f-5913-b2e5-1daf8eefa8db'}, {'abstract': 'David E. Hoffman’s “The Billion Dollar Spy,” No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list, traces the Cold War exploits of a Soviet military engineer who for years passed secrets to the C.I.A.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'David E. Hoffman’s “The Billion Dollar Spy,” No. 13 on the hardcover nonfiction list, traces the Cold War exploits of a Soviet military engineer who for years passed secrets to the C.I.A.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Secret Agent Man: “The English Spy,” Daniel Silva’s latest espionage thriller to feature the art restorer and Israeli agent Gabriel Allon, drops out of the top spot in its second week on the hardcover fiction list, falling to No. 4. Meanwhile, a real-life spy story has climbed onto the hardcover nonfiction list: “The Billion Dollar Spy,” by the journalist and Pulitzer Prize-winning author David E. Hoffman, is new at No. 13. Hoffman’s book traces the Cold War exploits of Adolf Tolkachev, a Soviet military engineer who for years passed secrets to the C.I.A. “He put the documents in his coat,” Hoffman told NPR this month, “and he walked out of that top-secret institute at the lunch hour back to his apartment, which was a 20-minute walk, and he took out a camera and he photographed the pages and then he went back to work and put them back in the file. And he did this for years.” One way the book brings its subject to life, as Scott Shane noted in his review for The New York Times’s daily Arts section, is through the detail that Tolkachev asked his American handlers to pass along rock ’n’ roll for his teenage son, including Led Zeppelin, Pink Floyd and the Beatles. “My son, as many of his contemporaries in school, has a passion for Western music,” he explained in one letter. “I too, in spite of my age, like to listen to this music.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26insidelist-photo/26insidelist-photo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 455, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26insidelist-photo/26insidelist-photo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 455, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26insidelist-photo/26insidelist-photo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26insidelist-photo/26insidelist-photo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26insidelist-photo/26insidelist-photo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f73e849-05e4-5d2f-9a1a-3b0bbd586fc8', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f73e849-05e4-5d2f-9a1a-3b0bbd586fc8'}, {'abstract': 'Two books of reporting from North Korea offer details about daily life and the changing economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/north-korea-confidential-and-north-korea-undercover.html', 'snippet': 'Two books of reporting from North Korea offer details about daily life and the changing economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'He was the Tammany Hall boss of North Korea, the man who dispensed favors and stayed close to the ruling Kim family, so close he married the daughter of the nation’s founder. He got rich by selling commodities to China: low-end coal and probably the more valuable rare earths. He ran the Ministry of People’s Security. Those gilded connections did not shield one of North Korea’s most powerful men from the wrath of the young new leader, the 30-something Kim Jong-un, and for reasons that no one outside the inner sanctums of Pyongyang knows, Jang Song-thaek suffered a bloody downfall.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19PERLEZ/19PERLEZ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘North Korea Confidential’ and ‘North Korea Undercover’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Strange Land', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perlez, Jane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sweeney, John (1958- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tudor, Daniel (Journalist)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearson, James (Journalist)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'North Korea Confidential: Private Markets, Fashion Trends, Prison Camps, Dissenters and Defectors (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "North Korea Undercover: Inside the World's Most Secret State (Book)", 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Perlez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perlez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8435b9e4-b1e6-56a2-8852-f39b3ae9a854', 'word_count': 1107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8435b9e4-b1e6-56a2-8852-f39b3ae9a854'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE UNRAVELING: High Hopes and Missed Opportunities in Iraq, by Emma Sky. (PublicAffairs, $28.99.) The Briton who was the political adviser to American Gen. Ray Odierno from 2007 to 2010 offers an important and disturbing memoir.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/324efc62-e193-5527-80ca-8398755683b6', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/324efc62-e193-5527-80ca-8398755683b6'}, {'abstract': 'A debut novel centers on a father who thinks he will soon die.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/let-me-explain-you-by-annie-liontas.html', 'snippet': 'A debut novel centers on a father who thinks he will soon die.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Annie Liontas’s debut novel, “Let Me Explain You,” opens with a type of communication that many of us know intimately: an email from a parent. This one follows many of the comedic conventions of the form. There is the gnomic subject line, the unorthodox punctuation, the needlessly formal greeting and sign off. There is a reckless use of the “cc” function, and a \xadCASUALLY idiosyncratic \xadRELIANCE on THE CAPS lock KEY.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19YOUNG/19YOUNG-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 447, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19YOUNG/19YOUNG-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 447, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19YOUNG/0719-BKS-Young-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19YOUNG/0719-BKS-Young-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19YOUNG/0719-BKS-Young-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Let Me Explain You,’ by Annie Liontas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Exit Plan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Molly', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Liontas, Annie (1981- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Let Me Explain You (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Young', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Young', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f40391f-e878-5887-a784-bff31fb51083', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f40391f-e878-5887-a784-bff31fb51083'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Lovesey’s new whodunit, “Down Among the Dead Men,” involves a professional car thief, a missing art teacher and two formidable women.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/foul-players.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Lovesey’s new whodunit, “Down Among the Dead Men,” involves a professional car thief, a missing art teacher and two formidable women.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’ll it be today? A knotty puzzle mystery? A fast-paced police procedural? Something more high-toned, with a bit of wit? With the British author Peter Lovesey, there’s no need to make those agonizing decisions, because his books have it all. Take DOWN AMONG THE DEAD MEN (Soho Crime, $27.95), his new mystery featuring Peter Diamond, detective superintendent of the Bath constabulary. The story opens on a light note, with a professional car thief (call him “Driveaway Danny”) updating his skills with a remarkable gizmo that prevents a car from being locked — only to discover that the silver BMW he’s stolen has a body in the trunk.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/19CRIME-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/19CRIME-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 769}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/19CRIME-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 800, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/19CRIME-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1785, 'width': 1393, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/0719-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/0719-BKS-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19CRIME/0719-BKS-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Foul Players', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Foul Players', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovesey, Peter', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dahl, Julia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eriksson, Kjell', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panowich, Brian (1971- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Open Grave (Book)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Run You Down (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Bull Mountain (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Down Among the Dead Men (Book)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/64650a1b-029a-577a-8375-de492e212fc6', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64650a1b-029a-577a-8375-de492e212fc6'}, {'abstract': 'A Glasgow prostitute dumps her pimp and becomes a nanny.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/rise-by-karen-campbell.html', 'snippet': 'A Glasgow prostitute dumps her pimp and becomes a nanny.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Scottish writer Karen Campbell’s new novel begins with its heroine, Justine, making a panicked escape from the Glasgow home she shares with her psychotic pimp-cum-boyfriend, Charlie Boy. On a randomly chosen bus, heading in the general direction of Oban, she tries not to fidget, but her legs itch desperately — because her jeans are lined with stolen 10 and 50 pound notes. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 656}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1093, 'width': 999, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19GEE/0719-BKS---Gee-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Rise,’ by Karen Campbell', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Onward and Upward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Campbell, Karen (1967- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Rise (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sophie Gee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sophie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f77aa8b-1b1b-56d8-b1e0-59c5b31672b7', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f77aa8b-1b1b-56d8-b1e0-59c5b31672b7'}, {'abstract': 'A novel tracks a self-determined woman involved with the reintroduction of the gray wolf in England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/the-wolf-border-by-sarah-hall.html', 'snippet': 'A novel tracks a self-determined woman involved with the reintroduction of the gray wolf in England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The English zoologist Rachel Caine doesn’t have relationships: “Just sex.” Her mother had been the same way. When Rachel finds herself pregnant after one drunken night with Kyle, her longtime friend and colleague at a wolf recovery program in the Idaho wilds, she can’t face the situation — at least not as a couple. Her panicked flight brings her back home to the Lake District, to help with a powerful aristocrat’s quixotic scheme to reintroduce gray wolves to Britain. Her own reintroduction proves just as complicated and dangerous. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19MARLER/19MARLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Wolf Border,’ by Sarah Hall', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Territorial Lines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Sarah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Wolf Border (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Regina Marler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Regina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/83a0616f-998b-5a29-a93a-6cb0391d6205', 'word_count': 619, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83a0616f-998b-5a29-a93a-6cb0391d6205'}, {'abstract': 'A character writes a history of a Jewish neighborhood in Memphis in Steve Stern’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/books/review/the-pinch-by-steve-stern.html', 'snippet': 'A character writes a history of a Jewish neighborhood in Memphis in Steve Stern’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If your name is Stern and you seek literary attention, the evidence suggests you ought to consider a pseudonym. When Richard Stern — the brainy, digressive Chicago novelist and story writer who was better known for being unknown than for anything he wrote — died two years ago, his burden passed to Steve Stern, a fellow chronicler of Jewish experience via deeply schooled stories that can’t seem to get reviewed without reference to the author being not “as well known as he should be.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/19FISHMAN-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 734, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/19FISHMAN-SUB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 734}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/19FISHMAN-SUB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/19FISHMAN-SUB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1854, 'width': 1515, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/0719-BKS-FISHMAN-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/0719-BKS-FISHMAN-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/books/review/19FISHMAN-SUB/0719-BKS-FISHMAN-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The Pinch,’ by Steve Stern', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'World of Our Authors', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fishman, Boris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stern, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T16:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Boris Fishman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Boris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fishman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc1ade3d-fef2-596e-b43f-4c9d49ffeba1', 'word_count': 1179, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc1ade3d-fef2-596e-b43f-4c9d49ffeba1'}, {'abstract': 'On the occasion of the first birthday of his New York outpost Book/Shop, the store’s owner Erik Heywood selects eight titles inspired by the east and west coasts.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/17/summer-reading-list-bookshop-new-york/', 'snippet': 'On the occasion of the first birthday of his New York outpost Book/Shop, the store’s owner Erik Heywood selects eight titles inspired by the east and west coasts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Book/Shop’s New York location at 2 Bond Street turns one this week. Tucked into a bright corner inside the men’s clothing store C’H’C’M, it’s less a bookshop and more a shop about books. More interesting, perhaps, than its hard-to-find titles are its bibliophilic oddities: a Spanish book brush for dusting, Absorene Paper & Book Cleaner for maintenance and hand-sewn canvas book sleeves for travel. Minimal yet lively graphic prints (related to reading, of course) hang on the wall of the shop — which is lined with unique, morsel-size slotted bookshelves, all designed in-house.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16books-beddie-1/16books-beddie-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16books-beddie-1/16books-beddie-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16books-beddie-1/16books-beddie-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16books-beddie-1/16books-beddie-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/16/t-magazine/16books-beddie-1/16books-beddie-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subT