timedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/6727b11b-6771-5866-9293-5572c27ab525', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/6727b11b-6771-5866-9293-5572c27ab525'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Mattera, a Staten Island diver, takes a break from searching for sunken treasure by visiting parks with his children, taking walks and getting cocktails with his wife.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/19/nyregion/how-john-mattera-shipwreck-explorer-spends-his-sundays.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Mattera, a Staten Island diver, takes a break from searching for sunken treasure by visiting parks with his children, taking walks and getting cocktails with his wife.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I find shipwrecks,” John Mattera said the other day, when asked to describe what he does for a living. No, really, that’s what he does: search for sunken treasure. Mr. Mattera, 53, a former nightclub owner, bodyguard, moneylender and Staten Island tough guy, is a subject of Robert Kurson’s new book, “Pirate Hunters: Treasure, Obsession and the Search for a Legendary Pirate Ship,” about a quest for 17th-century booty in the seas around the Dominican Republic. It’s a yarn with plenty of guns and danger and breathless prose. Between dives, Mr. Mattera stays closer to home, doing research and writing and — along with his wife, Carolina — raising their three children, ages 8 months to 4, in the Great Kills section of Staten Island. His daughter from his previous marriage lives nearby.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/19/nyregion/19ROUTINE3/19ROUTINE3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 532, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/19/nyregion/19ROUTINE