', 'value': 'Wang, Honji', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-17T20:03:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/38a82871-2211-5e11-b0a4-ee4fe8fd50ec', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38a82871-2211-5e11-b0a4-ee4fe8fd50ec'}, {'abstract': 'The narrow victory shows that serious opposition is finally emerging against the chaebols, the powerful family-run conglomerates that dominate in South Korea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/18/business/dealbook/merger-vote-at-samsung-is-not-the-end-for-the-lee-family.html', 'snippet': 'The narrow victory shows that serious opposition is finally emerging against the chaebols, the powerful family-run conglomerates that dominate in South Korea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Lee family behind Samsung must be breathing a heavy sigh of relief. Its plans for an $8 billion merger of two Samsung affiliates, Samsung C&T and Cheil Industries, was narrowly approved on Friday by shareholders. The move came despite strong opposition from by Elliott Management, the hedge fund led by Paul E. Singer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Merger Vote at Samsung Is Not the End for the Lee Family', 'kicker': 'Breakingviews', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cheil Industries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Samsung Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Elliott Management Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'na