LOLITA (1962) An aging intellectual (James Mason) falls in love with a teenager (Sue Lyon) in Stanley Kubrick’s adaptation of the novel by Vladimir Nabokov. Because of the novel’s depiction of pedophilia “Lolita” was considered unfilmable until Kubrick and Nabokov came up with a screenplay that could pass the Production Code Administration and the Legion of Decency, opening the door to other cinematic treatments of sexual perversion. “How did they ever make a movie of ‘Lolita’?” Bosley Crowther asked in The New York Times. “They didn’t.” But “once the reader has been advised not to expect the distractingly sultry climate and sardonic mischievousness of the book,” he added, “it must be said that Mr. Kubrick has got a lot of fun and frolic in his film.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Saturday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-18T05:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4235d3ee-7061-5276-a5e0-f2fc06b0cba1', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4235d3ee-7061-5276-a5e0-f2fc06b0cba1'}, {'abstract': 'While some readers in Japan approved of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s plan to restore combat powers to the military, many others strongly objected.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/18/world/asia/shinzo-abes-military-plan-draws-sharp-response-from-readers.html', 'snippet': 'While some readers in Japan 