Japanese Parliament last week brought Prime Minister Shinzo Abe a step closer to one of his most important national security goals — a law that would give the Japanese armed forces limited powers to engage in foreign combat for the first time since World War II. But the way Mr. Abe engineered the victory has caused great anxiety about whether he intends to honor Japan’s deep postwar commitment to pacifism.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 679, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1358, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/opinion/20mon1/20mon1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Wrestles With Its Pacifism', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Wrestles With Its Pacifism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-20T07:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f82e119b-2c9c-510c-a001-4c0775c69e22', 'word_count': 621, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f82e119b-2c9c-510c-a001-4c0775c69e22'}, {'abstract': 'After nearly bringing down the economy in 2008, how is it possible that Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac are still around?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/20/opinion/fannie-and-freddie-are-back-bigger-and-badder-than-ever.html', 'snippet': 'After nearly bringing down the economy in 2008, how is it possible that Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac are still around?', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER the financial crisis of 2008, there was one thing that almost everyone agreed on. The government-sponsored mortgage giants, Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, had to go. While shareholders and executives reaped the profits from Fannie and Freddie in good times, taxpayers were stuck with the bill in a crisis. President Obama described their dysfunctional business model as “Heads we win, tails you lose.” But here we are, seven years after the crisis, and nothing has changed.', 