iddlename': 'Ross', 'lastname': 'Sorkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/015b7d04-8774-532e-810d-40004ae16e2e', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/015b7d04-8774-532e-810d-40004ae16e2e'}, {'abstract': 'After three hearings in Tehran, the fourth was likely to be the last for the Washington Post correspondent, although no date has been set.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/21/world/middleeast/iran-reporters-espionage-trial-may-be-nearing-end-editor-says.html', 'snippet': 'After three hearings in Tehran, the fourth was likely to be the last for the Washington Post correspondent, although no date has been set.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The secretive trial of Jason Rezaian, the Washington Post’s Tehran correspondent, who has been imprisoned for nearly a year, may soon be over, the newspaper’s executive editor, Martin Baron, said Monday. In a statement, he said the correspondent’s Iranian lawyer, Leila Ahsan, had been told that after three hearings in Tehran over the past two months, the fourth was likely to be the last, although no date has been set. Mr. Rezaian faces up to 20 years in prison on charges that include espionage. He has asserted his innocence since his arrest last July 22, and Ms. Ahsan has said there is no evidence to support the charges. Mr. Baron, who has denounced the prosecution as ludicrous, said in the statement that “it is long past time for the Iranian authorities to bring this process to an end.” Mr. Rezaian is one of at least three Americans held in Iran, an issue that has become more pressing to their relatives and supporters since an international deal on the country’s disputed nuclear activities was reached last week, potentially easing the estranged relations between Washington and Tehran. Also on Monday, the Committee to Protect Journalists, a press advocacy group, published a letter  to Sadeq Larijani, the head of Iran’s judiciary, asking him to “ensure that this case is resolved immediately.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Reporter’s Espionage Trial May Be Nearing End, Editor Says', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Middle East; Iran: Reporter’s Espionage Trial May Be Nearing End, Editor Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rezaian, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baron, Martin D', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Prisoners', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-21T01:37:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0ce336e-17de-5110-8873-9a89b993802f', 'word_count': 218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0ce336e-17de-5110-8873-9a89b993802f'}, {'abstract': 'Kim Barker describes how she stumbled upon a Manhattan McDonald’s with a dark side.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2015/07/20/finding-the-junkie-mcdonalds-and-drug-culture-in-manhattan/', 'snippet': 'Kim Barker describes how she stumbled upon a Manhattan McDonald’s with a dark side.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I first went to the McDonald’s on Eighth Avenue in late October 2014, to meet a man known as “Loosey Tone,” because he sold loose Newport cigarettes for 50 cents apiece and his first name was Tony. Loosey, who couldn’t read and couldn’t write and had a speech impediment from birth, was helping me with an article about his landlord, Yury Baumblit, who was trying to evict him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/20/blogs/mcdonalds/mcdonalds-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding the ‘Junkie McDonald’s’ and Drug Culture in Manhattan', 'kicker': 'Times