'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0550833-5397-535f-b078-5ca2f3254d3b', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0550833-5397-535f-b078-5ca2f3254d3b'}, {'abstract': 'Walter Dundervill’s work is three-hour installation piece at Jack, in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/arts/dance/arena-builds-and-crumbles-a-temporary-civilization-through-dance.html', 'snippet': 'Walter Dundervill’s work is three-hour installation piece at Jack, in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The beauty of ancient sites lies partly in their decay. We call them “ruins” with affection; it’s the pillar intact and the pillar crumbled, side by side, that tell a compelling story. The multidisciplinary dance artist Walter Dundervill is something of an archaeologist of self-made worlds where grace sits at the edge of destruction. He layers historical references on top of one another, places them in colorful, immersive environments of his own design and populates them with splendidly clad characters. Then he starts excavating. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/arts/26GUIDE7/26GUIDE7-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/26/arts/26GUIDE7/26GUIDE7-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/arts/26GUIDE7/26GUIDE7-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/arts/26GUIDE7/26GUIDE7-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJu