, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/23/arts/23SOMOGYI/23SOMOGYI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 964, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/23/arts/23SOMOGYI/23SOMOGYI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1928, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/23/arts/23SOMOGYI/23SOMOGYI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/23/arts/23SOMOGYI/23SOMOGYI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/23/arts/23SOMOGYI/23SOMOGYI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Farewell Is in View for Jennie Somogyi, a City Ballet Lifer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Farewell Show Is in View for a Lifer at City Ballet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cooper, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balanchine, George', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Somogyi, Jennie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-22T19:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be358e07-2bfb-5ea4-917b-7e13a8449710', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be358e07-2bfb-5ea4-917b-7e13a8449710'}, {'abstract': 'Alice Anderson discusses the significance of various copper-wire-bound works on view in her new show in London.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/22/alice-anderson-memory-movement-memory-objects/', 'snippet': 'Alice Anderson discusses the significance of various copper-wire-bound works on view in her new show in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There have been moments in the career of Alice Anderson when it felt as though her flame-red hair might just eclipse her. It is, after all, the artist’s constant inspiration, fantastically rendered time and again by her deft hands in copper wire. But her new show, “Memory Movement Memory Objects,” deflects the focus from her own physiognomy and out into the wider world — albeit in a familiarly burnished hue. In a throwback to childhood days spent playing with bobbins and unpicking hems, the London sculptor has turned again to the threadlike medium of copper wire to painstakingly mummify some 100 everyday objects as disparate as record players and human bones — but has left the work of covering a 1967 Ford Mustang to the show’s visitors. In her own words, she tells T the significance of a sample of these precious, tightly bound treasures, which are on view at the Wellcome Collection in London beginning today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Mouth of an Artist Whose Own Fiery Hair Is Her Inspiration', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-22T19:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aimee Farrell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aimee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farrell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0287d315-6747-547f-a351-805059022ae7', 'word_count': 176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0287d315-6747-547f-a351-805059022ae7'}, {'abstract': 'This week, a children’s book made a rare appearance on the cover of The New York Times Book Review. But the book is not just any children’s book and Pamela Paul, the section’s editor, explains why Dr. Seuss’ posthumous “What Pet Should I Get?” earned the cover review.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/times-insider/2015/07/22/dr-seuss-and-the-book-review-remind-us-of-the-joys-of-childhood-reading/', 'snippet': 'This week, a children’s book made a rare appearance on the cover of The New York Times Book Review. But the book is not just any children’s book and Pamela Paul, the section’s editor, explains why Dr. Seuss’ posthumous “What Pet Should I Get?” ear...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, for the first time ever, the Book Review featured a picture book on its cover — a book that, for all of its 32 pages, holds fewer words than the first page of an average novel. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/22/blogs/Insider-BRCover/Insider-BRCover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 636, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/22/blogs/Insider-BRCover/Insider-BRCover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 636}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/22/blogs/Insider-BRCover/Insider-BRCover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 966, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name