r': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper West Side (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Condominiums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-23T08:58:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a20dd755-eba8-5630-b87e-1afcf709858a', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a20dd755-eba8-5630-b87e-1afcf709858a'}, {'abstract': 'Female writers don’t need to destroy the world in order to imagine feeling unsafe in it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/books/review/its-the-end-of-the-world-as-she-knows-it.html', 'snippet': 'Female writers don’t need to destroy the world in order to imagine feeling unsafe in it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ninety-nine percent of the world’s population has been wiped out. From Stephen King’s “The Stand” to Peter Heller’s “The Dog Stars,” this is the number most favored by authors of dystopian and post-apocalyptic fiction. It’s a clean way of conveying that we shouldn’t expect anyone to receive concerned calls from mom and dad but also an efficient means of narrowing the stage of human violence.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s the End of the World as She Knows It', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Take', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s the End of the World as She Knows It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crosley, Sloane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-23T09:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sloane Crosley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sloane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crosley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7a21750-5bfd-52e0-8a6b-0edc62840879', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7a21750-5bfd-52e0-8a6b-0edc62840879'}, {'abstract': 'The Texas senator and presidential candidate on his favorite comics and his preferred captain of the U.S.S. Enterprise.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/magazine/ted-cruz-is-more-of-a-spider-man-guy.html', 'snippet': 'The Texas senator and presidential candidate on his favorite comics and his preferred captain of the U.S.S. Enterprise.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your new book is called ‘‘A Time for Truth: Reigniting the Promise of America.’’ A few months ago you were in the news for warning a little girl that ‘‘the world is on fire.’’ Do you believe setting things on fire is good or bad? Both. I am a passionate believer in the fire of liberty. Indeed, I have encouraged young people to become arsonists, spreading the fire of liberty. Now that said, the world being on fire is not a good thing.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 1334, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1334}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201