pitch meeting that never ends: “It’s ‘Twin Peaks’ meets ‘The Twilight Zone’ meets ‘The Truman Show’ meets ‘Lost’…” To that list of titles, you could add at least two more — “The Village,” which shares the “Wayward” premise of a town nobody can leave, and “The Happening,” which anticipated Mother Nature’s harsh response to environmental tinkering. So it makes sense that M. Night Shyamalan, who directed both of those films, would make “Wayward Pines” his first television foray, directing the pilot and executive producing the rest.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'M. Night Shyamalan on Endings and the ‘Wayward Pines’ Series Finale', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Crouch, Blake (1978- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shyamalan, M Night', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-23T15:50:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Tobias', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tobias', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b041081-264c-5270-9771-562bbbbe53f7', 'word_count': 823, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b041081-264c-5270-9771-562bbbbe53f7'}, {'abstract': 'An Irma Stern painting that was used as a family’s kitchen bulletin board will be auctioned at Bonhams, with an estimate of $1.1 million to $1.5 million.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/23/painting-used-as-a-kitchen-bulletin-board-estimated-to-bring-1-1-million/', 'snippet': 'An Irma Stern painting that was used as a family’s kitchen bulletin board will be auctioned at Bonhams, with an estimate of $1.1 million to $1.5 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — A painting by the important South African artist Irma Stern, donated to raise funds for the defense in the 1961 treason trial of Nelson Mandela and his fellow African National Congress detainees, has been discovered in a London kitchen, The Guardian reported. Covered in letters, postcards and bills, it was being used as a bulletin board until spotted by Hannah O’Leary from the auction house Bonhams, during a routine valuation in early May.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23artsbeat-stern/23artsbeat-stern-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 803, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23artsbeat-stern/23artsbeat-stern-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 803}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23artsbeat-stern/23artsbeat-stern-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 766, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23artsbeat-stern/23artsbeat-stern-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1531, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23artsbeat-stern/23artsbeat-stern-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23artsbeat-stern/23artsbeat-stern-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/23ar