Newport Folk Festival audiences — by abandoning an acoustic guitar for a Fender Stratocaster — and analyzes why it really did matter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/24/books/review-dylan-goes-electric-considers-folk-rock-and-a-60s-divide.html', 'snippet': 'Elijah Wald’s book revisits the night in 1965 that Bob Dylan shocked Newport Folk Festival audiences — by abandoning an acoustic guitar for a Fender Stratocaster — and analyzes why it really did matter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturday night is the 50th anniversary of Dylanageddon: the night Bob Dylan savaged the Newport Folk Festival by making loud, electrified noise at a sanctuary that had never been thus sullied. The story of his 1965 assault on Newport is very well known. Its effects have been contemplated ad nauseam. Its details show up in every Dylan biography. It’s so essential to the Dylan story that it may even have engendered folk songs of its own. So the idea of a book to commemorate this geezer milestone seems unnecessary, to put it kindly. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/24BOOK/24BOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 893, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/24BOOK/24BOOK-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 893}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/24BOOK/24BOOK-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 689, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/24/arts/24BOOK/24BOOK-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1377, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption