lead_paragraph': 'PERFIDIA, by James Ellroy. (Vintage, $16.95.) After a Japanese family is murdered in 1940s Los Angeles, a police force with questionable ethics is tasked with solving the case, blurring the lines between criminality and morality. As our reviewer, Dennis Lehane, wrote, “The police are not knights, they’re occupiers, and in ‘Perfidia,’ Ellroy comes closer than ever to making the case that he writes alt-histories not of the Los Angeles police but of the Los Angeles police state.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-24T16:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joumana Khatib', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joumana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khatib', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/139f8b27-d594-55ec-b0d9-7bd3a14d176e', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/139f8b27-d594-55ec-b0d9-7bd3a14d176e'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist looks at the challenges involved in keeping the world from going hungry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/books/review/the-end-of-plenty-by-joel-k-bourne-jr.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist looks at the challenges involved in keeping the world from going hungry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An 18th-century question matters for 21st-century foodies: “Are human beings perfectible?” If our species can improve itself, if we can learn to consume less, have fewer children and wean ourselves off meat, the planet might yet flourish. If not, the fear is that we’ll breed and eat ourselves into extinction. Many have addressed this quandary, but Robert Malthus’s ideas are the ones Joel Bourne puts at the heart of “The End of Plenty,” his big book on the global food system.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1352, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/26PATEL/26PATEL-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘The End of Plenty,’ by Joel K. Bourne Jr.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mouths to Feed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bourne, Joel K Jr (1962- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-24T16:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raj Patel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fcf45c9-ce16-55e0-b29f-1333f70a7ab5', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fcf45c9-ce16-55e0-b29f-1333f70a7ab5'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in David Gates’s stories engage in festivals of bad decisions. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/books/review/a-hand-reached-down-to-guide-me-by-david-gates.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in David Gates’s stories engage in festivals of bad decisions. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world of the knowing and frank stories in David Gates’s new collection is located north of New York City, ranging roughly from Peekskill to Vermont. The bright, educated, sardonic people who inhabit this world call themselves refugees from New York, dismissing their new locale as Olde Quaintsburgh or the boonies. Full of rueful badinage, like the characters in his novels “Jernigan” and “Preston Falls,” they make for witty and cautionary company. “A Hand Reached Down to Guide Me” is a smart book about smart, articulate people who get in their own way again and again, engaging in episodic festivals of bad decisions and then talking about their plight with arresting candor, in language rife with humor and allusion.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-Carlso