erman vegetarian in the early 1900s tries to found a colony in the South Seas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If, while sprawled in a deck chair or on the beach this summer, you crave a book whose tone and emotional landscape mirror your own state of torpor and cosseted relaxation, such a book would not be “Imperium.” Although this very amusing and bracingly oddball novel by the Swiss writer Christian Kracht does feature several palm-covered islands — not to mention many gallons of coconut oil and copious amounts of undress — calling it a beach read is like calling “Psycho” maternal.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 481, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 481}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1404, 'width': 1750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-ALFORD/0726-BKS-ALFORD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Christian Kracht’s ‘Imperium’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Commune Under the Palms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alford, Henry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kracht, Christian (1966- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Imperium: A Fiction of the South Seas (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-24T16:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Henry Alford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Henry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3543c276-7675-55e4-8fe2-9b9f49790495', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3543c276-7675-55e4-8fe2-9b9f49790495'}, {'abstract': 'After an inescapable loss, a cosmetics executive moves his family to London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/books/review/the-loved-ones-by-mary-beth-hughes.html', 'snippet': 'After an inescapable loss, a cosmetics executive moves his family to London.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Only a flurry. A thin blanket of white, something to take the gray mounds of snow and make them new.” So begins Mary-Beth Hughes’s third book, “The Loved Ones.” It is 1969, and Jean Devlin is leaving her house on the Jersey Shore in “the Valiant wagon she used for errands.” The departure is fraught, partly because the fraughtness of departure is one of the book’s themes, and partly because of the nature of the driveway. “Jean made the reverse up the steep curve, then backed into the roadside pull-off where most delivery drivers chickened out and parked. They’d rather hand carry a refrigerator than make the plunge, but Jean could do it blindfolded.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 514, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 514}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 878, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1525, 'width': 1779, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/books/review/0726-BKS-BELLER/0726-BKS-BELLER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, '