: 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-24T17:49:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roxane Gay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roxane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/477780da-074a-586d-9996-27caafd9fc47', 'word_count': 1043, 'uri': 'nyt://article/477780da-074a-586d-9996-27caafd9fc47'}, {'abstract': 'With financing secured, work will begin on Essex Crossing, a 1.65 million-square-foot project that will add 1,000 housing units, half of which will be permanently affordable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/26/realestate/new-mixed-income-housing-onthe-lower-east-side.html', 'snippet': 'With financing secured, work will begin on Essex Crossing, a 1.65 million-square-foot project that will add 1,000 housing units, half of which will be permanently affordable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For nearly half a century, the view south from the corner of Essex and Delancey Streets has been of vacant parking lots behind chain-link fences, carving a desolate hole in the otherwise vibrant neighborhood. But this month, construction finally began on a $1 billion redevelopment project that will reshape the Lower East Side.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/26/realestate/26SEWARD/26SEWARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/26/realestate/26SEWARD/26SEWARD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image