'pub_date': '2015-07-26T21:32:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/977e639c-4224-5a39-9e58-0b2222400565', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/977e639c-4224-5a39-9e58-0b2222400565'}, {'abstract': 'Lil Buck’s limber display and the equality among performers in Ms. Dorrance’s show were memorable at this dance festival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/27/arts/dance/lil-buck-and-michelle-dorrance-dance-enliven-lincoln-center-out-of-doors.html', 'snippet': 'Lil Buck’s limber display and the equality among performers in Ms. Dorrance’s show were memorable at this dance festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friday and Saturday nights offered, at Damrosch Park as part of the Lincoln Center Out of Doors series, performances by Dorrance Dance (tap) and Lil Buck (the style called Memphis jookin) that were, in their different ways, phenomenal. But Dorrance Dance left an impression of marvelously developed plenitude, with a program that ideally celebrated and renewed the resources of its genre, whereas Lil Buck looked like an artistically under-realized genius, a dazzling prodigy whose gifts are in danger of turning into point-scoring stunts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/27/arts/27LILBUC/27LILBUC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/27/arts/27LILBUC/27LILBUC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 