ank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsch, Jesse', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-29T04:24:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Harms', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harms', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50a7224e-2492-5cb0-acff-adc493b92351', 'word_count': 658, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50a7224e-2492-5cb0-acff-adc493b92351'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/29/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '3 P.M. (FX Movie Channel) HEREAFTER (2010) Clint Eastwood imagines what happens after people die by braiding together three stories, each involving a collision between the living and the dead. In San Francisco, George Lonegan (Matt Damon), who receives messages from the deceased loved ones of anyone he touches, chooses a life of obscurity instead of exploiting his gift. In London, a boy feels the presence of his twin, whose death has left him adrift. (The brothers are played by George and Frankie McLaren.) And in Paris, Marie Lelay (Cécile de France), who nearly died in the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami, is convinced she has seen a metaphysical reality the world is determined to ignore. “Persuasion is not really the point, though if anyone could make me believe in ghosts, it would be Clint Eastwood,” A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “What does seem new — newly strange, newly beautiful — is what ‘Hereafter’ makes of the here and now. It is a curious movie in both senses of the word: an unusual experience and an open-ended inquiry into something nobody can really claim to understand. It leaves you wondering, which may be the most fitting way of saying that it’s wonderful.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on TV Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What&#8217;s On TV Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-29T05:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/4371694b-ed68-5f79-a5fd-676ce34dfb3b', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4371694b-ed68-5f79-a5fd-676ce34dfb3b'}, {'abstract': 'Corrections appearing in print on Wednesday, July 29, 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/29/pageoneplus/corrections-july-29-2015.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on Wednesday, July 29, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: July 29, 2015', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-29T06:29:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b78d2583-2a43-5681-a9e0-265f1a8a88c8', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b78d2583-2a43-5681-a9e0-265f1a8a88c8'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, July 29, 2015.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/29/pageoneplus/quotation-of-the-day.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, July 29, 2015.', 'lead_paragraph': '“We’ve got a lot of people in prison, frankly, that don’t really in my view need to be there.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2015-07-29T06:30:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae6ae5a4-3c79-50d1-ae0b-f81e4f227189', 'word_count': 46, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae6ae5a4-3c79-50d1-ae0b-f81e4f227189'}, {'abstract': 'When residents of a polluted village went to complain to the government, an official appeared to be so drunk that he was not able to offer much of a response, Henan Television reported. The display was captured on video and posted online.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/29/after-appearing-drunk-on-television-chinese-official-faces-inquiry/', 'snippet': 'When residents of a polluted village went to complain to the government, an official appeared to be so drunk that he was not able to offer much of a response, Henan Television reported. The display was captured on video and posted online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With more than 10 cement plants grinding up stone, the village of Goutou in the central Chinese province of Henan has a serious problem with dust and lung disease risk, according to a Henan Television report. So after a 46-year-old villager died last week of causes believed to be linked to the pollution, residents went to complain to a local official.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Appearing Drunk on Television, Chinese Official Faces Inquiry', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo