ial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0064519f-4e34-52fd-82b3-9c2432090a57', 'word_count': 892, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0064519f-4e34-52fd-82b3-9c2432090a57'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2015/07/29/classical-playlist-hindemith-shostakovich-and-dutilleux/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HINDEMITH: ‘The Long Christmas Dinner’Camille Zamora, Sara Murphy, Jarrett Ott and other singers; American Symphony Orchestra; Leon Botstein, conductor(Bridge Records)Paul Hindemith rightly saw rich operatic potential in Thornton Wilder’s one-act play “The Long Christmas Dinner,” first performed at Yale University Theater in 1931. Wilder’s delicate, profound work follows the story of an American family over 90 years through periodic visits to Christmas dinners at the household table. Wilder, an astute music lover, readily adapted his play into a libretto for Hindemith, cutting the text considerably and providing some new rhymed lyrics for extended vocal pieces. Though well received, Hindemith’s opera, completed in 1961, has become a rarity. The conductor Leon Botstein came to the rescue in 2014 when he conducted a fine cast and the American Symphony Orchestra in a beguiling staged production at Alice Tully Hall. (This fascinating program preceded the opera with a performance of the Wilder play by a strong cast of actors, using the same set.) Bridge Records has now released a live recording of the performance. We hear the highs and lows of the Bayard family discussed over Christmas dinners, starting from the first at the family’s new house, when the matriarchal Mother Bayard beams as her son, who runs the family firm, slices turkey alongside his cheerful wife. Cousins come and go; children are born and have children of their own; one son dies in war; a child dies in infancy; an unmarried cousin comes to live; social mores and customs change. It’s a special opera, and this performance sounds as good as I remembered it to be. (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1258, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/12/22/arts/CHRISTMAS/CHRISTMAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Classical Playlist: Hindemith, Shostakovich and Dutilleux', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 