me': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Translation and Interpreters', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-30T12:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/14fb61d3-286b-50ec-a353-3781c708359b', 'word_count': 1246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14fb61d3-286b-50ec-a353-3781c708359b'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Chávez (1899-1978), the subject of this summer’s Bard Music Festival, made his mark as a composer, conductor and bureaucrat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/02/arts/music/carlos-chavez-mexican-modernist.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Chávez (1899-1978), the subject of this summer’s Bard Music Festival, made his mark as a composer, conductor and bureaucrat.', 'lead_paragraph': '“European musicians are of the worst kind,” the composer Carlos Chávez declared in a 1931 letter. “Conductors, pianists, violinists, singers and so on are ‘prima donna’ minded people — they are very important to themselves. We must change the situation, Aaron.” In a reply to his close friend, the young Aaron Copland concurred: “All you wrote about music in America awoke a responsive echo in my heart. I am through with Europe, Carlos, and I believe as you do, that our salvation must come from ourselves and that we must fight the foreign element in American music.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/arts/02CHAVEZ1/02CHAVEZ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 381, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/02/arts/02CHAVEZ1/02CHAVEZ1-arti