ge Crisis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-30T19:49:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'DealBook', 'byline': {'original': 'By William D. Cohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51632e5d-8a1d-5ddf-976f-26acb5d1feae', 'word_count': 1000, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51632e5d-8a1d-5ddf-976f-26acb5d1feae'}, {'abstract': '“Works From the 1960s and 1970s” includes Mr. Valentine’s plastic sculptures “Circle Blue Smoke Flow” and “Double Column Gray.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/31/arts/design/review-de-wain-valentine-show-at-david-zwirner.html', 'snippet': '“Works From the 1960s and 1970s” includes Mr. Valentine’s plastic sculptures “Circle Blue Smoke Flow” and “Double Column Gray.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For sheer physical beauty, few objects in any New York gallery at the moment exceed this exhibition’s plastic sculptures, which were made in California by De Wain Valentine in the 1960s and ’70s. Associated with the West Coast movements known as Light and Space and Finish Fetish, Mr. Valentine cast his works in variously colored, translucent polyester resins, creating sleek, geometric forms that intimate transcendentalist vibes.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31VALENTINEGALLERY1/31VALENTINEGALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31VALENTINEGALLERY1/31VALENTINEGALLERY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 