ents for the amateur market.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 575, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/arts/31NIGHTMUSIC/31NIGHTMUSIC-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Review: Emanuel Ax, at the Mostly Mozart Festival, Gives a 19th-Century Lesson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'All Hands on Deck: Four on the Keys and One Turning the Pages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schweitzer, Vivien', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mostly Mozart Festival', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brahms, Johannes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schumann, Robert', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ax, Emanuel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weiss, Orion', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-31T01:12:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee895f11-be64-5c98-8e15-f48b177aebcd', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee895f11-be64-5c98-8e15-f48b177aebcd'}, {'abstract': 'A movie explores the uses, from artistic to therapeutic, of a ubiquitous toy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/31/movies/review-in-a-lego-brickumentary-master-builders-working-and-playing.html', 'snippet': 'A movie explores the uses, from artistic to therapeutic, of a ubiquitous toy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’d expect “A Lego Brickumentary” to be an extended plug for Lego products, and it is certainly that — to an extent. (Lego did not finance the film.) And this nimble, expertly paced family-friendly film by Daniel Junge and Kief Davidson dutifully covers the origins of the Lego company in Billund, Denmark, and its growth into a global toy brand rivaling Mattel and Hasbro. But it delves into other things: how engineers and psychologists have employed the pieces as tools, and the ways in which people have adopted Legos as a medium to realize their creative visions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype'