ns', 'value': 'Jerusalem (Israel)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bank', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Israeli Settlements', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-31T01:36:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f92573aa-f831-579c-9a81-d7b0762a1c45', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f92573aa-f831-579c-9a81-d7b0762a1c45'}, {'abstract': 'After two elections in which liberals sought to cast them as the secretive face of the G.O.P., the billionaire brothers want to change perceptions of their family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/07/31/us/koch-brothers-brave-spotlight-to-try-to-alter-their-image.html', 'snippet': 'After two elections in which liberals sought to cast them as the secretive face of the G.O.P., the billionaire brothers want to change perceptions of their family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once known for grim letters to fellow wealthy Americans warning of socialist apocalypse, Charles G. Koch now promotes research on the link between freedom and everyday happiness. Turn on “The Big Bang Theory” or “Morning Joe,” and you are likely to see soft-focus television spots introducing some of the many employees of Koch Industries. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/us/01JPKOCH1/01JPKOCH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 455, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/31/us/01JPKOCH1/01JPKOCH1-articleLarge.jpg', 'x