ct', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-31T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Upshot', 'section_name': 'The Upshot', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aaron E. Carroll', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Carroll', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a989e04-6096-5e26-a059-dae6d3c2e118', 'word_count': 1048, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a989e04-6096-5e26-a059-dae6d3c2e118'}, {'abstract': 'A report suggests that a program intended to help as many as four million troubled borrowers has allowed big banks to run roughshod over them again and again.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/02/business/pulling-down-underwater-borrowers.html', 'snippet': 'A report suggests that a program intended to help as many as four million troubled borrowers has allowed big banks to run roughshod over them again and again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Lucy Circe became disabled and could no longer work, she applied to Bank of America for a mortgage loan modification on her Vermont home. Over more than two years, starting in 2012, the bank repeatedly requested copies of documents that had already been provided, asked for proof that she was no longer married to a man she did not even know, and made other errors, like asking why Ms. Circe had indicated that she didn’t want to keep her property when she had actually told the bank she did.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Slack Lifeline for Drowning Homeowners', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Slack Lifeline for Drowning Homeowners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morgenson, Gretchen (Per)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-31T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Morgenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morgenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97eb7279-bdd7-530e-a531-20a3a72ef924', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97eb7279-bdd7-530e-a531-20a3a72ef924'}, {'abstract': 'The hotel wins high marks for its neighborhood feel and is a smart choice for travelers looking for an authentic sense of place away from the crowds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/02/travel/ludlow-nyc-hotel-review.html', 'snippet': 'The hotel wins high marks for its neighborhood feel and is a smart choice for travelers looking for an authentic sense of place away from the crowds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'From $295.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/travel/02CHECKIN1/02CHECKIN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 462, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/02/travel/02CHECKIN1/02CHECKIN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 462}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/tr