la-mclain.html', 'snippet': 'This novel is based on the life of Beryl Markham, horsewoman and aviator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you were half awake in 2011 (or 2012 or 2013), you couldn’t have avoided Paula McLain’s runaway best seller, “The Paris Wife.” That fictional autobiography of Ernest Hemingway’s beleaguered starter wife, Hadley Richardson, unfolds pretty much as the Hemingways’ marriage does, in Paris in the 1920s. Caught up in the mass hysteria, I too bought a copy. But when I came to this line, after just half a dozen pages, I couldn’t go on: “I don’t want to think and I don’t want to feel, either, unless it’s as simple as this beautiful boy’s knee inches from mine.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/2FULLER/2FULLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 782, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/2FULLER/2FULLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 782}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/2FULLER/2FULLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 786, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/2FULLER/2FULLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1571, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/2FULLER/2FULLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/2FULLER/2FULLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumb