': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8d08a7b-7475-515e-9eec-3a4fbc828517'}, {'abstract': 'Shohret Hoshur, a journalist who fled the Chinese authorities in Xinjiang, has continued to chronicle violence there for Radio Free Asia, based in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/01/world/asia/a-voice-from-chinas-uighur-homeland-reporting-from-the-united-states.html', 'snippet': 'Shohret Hoshur, a journalist who fled the Chinese authorities in Xinjiang, has continued to chronicle violence there for Radio Free Asia, based in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — IN December 1994, Shohret Hoshur, then a 29-year-old journalist in a remote corner of the far western Chinese region of Xinjiang, said goodbye to his homeland. After writing two articles in his native Uighur language that incensed the local authorities, he escaped using a false passport he bought for about $600.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/07/31/world/hoshur-web/hoshur-web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Voice From China’s Uighur Homeland, Reporting From the U.S.', 'kicker': 'The Saturday Profile', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reporting From the U.S. on China’s Uighur Homeland', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoshur, Shohret (1965- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Forsythe, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Uighurs (Chinese Ethnic Group)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Xinjiang (China)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Radio Free Asia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-31T15:17:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Forsythe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Forsythe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f34ed8d1-eb24-573d-8013-5070eddd795c', 'word_count': 1411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f34ed8d1-eb24-573d-8013-5070eddd795c'}, {'abstract': 'William Finnegan, who has written several sober examinations of the world’s hot spots, has his first best seller with the surfing memoir “Barbarian Days,” No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/09/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'William Finnegan, who has written several sober examinations of the world’s hot spots, has his first best seller with the surfing memoir “Barbarian Days,” No. 5 on the hardcover nonfiction list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Waves: As a war reporter and international correspondent for The New Yorker, William Finnegan has long been celebrated for his sober examinations of the world’s hot spots — going back to 1986, all of his books have been “editors’ choice” picks in the Book Review, including dispatches from apartheid-era South Africa, civil-war-torn Mozambique and poverty-stricken America. None of those books became best sellers. But his latest, the surfing memoir “Barbarian Days,” ends Finnegan’s dry spell at last, landing on the hardcover nonfiction list at No.\u202f5. “I was kind of nervous about coming out of the closet as a surfer,” Finnegan told The National Post in Canada last month. “I mean, I was a political reporter and writing a lot of political opinion pieces for The New Yorker, and I had a kind of reluctance to expose this part of my life and have a bunch of people say, ‘What do you know? You’re just a dumb surfer.’\u200a” Once he started, though, he couldn’t stop. “It’s easy for me to write about the act of surfing. I find it endlessly interesting,” he said. “The trick is to make it interesting to readers who couldn’t care less.” This columnist might be the kind of reader he means — I’m not a surfer, dumb or otherwise, although friends in California once coaxed me onto a board for 20 seconds. But I do kayak, and I recognize the giddy obsession Finnegan brings to his sport. In that sense, “Barbarian Days” offers the same heedless pleasure as any addiction memoir before the reckoning; it is, as Alice Gregory writes in The New York Review of Books, “an utterly convincing study in the joy of treating seriously an unserious thing.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-finnegan/09insidelist-finnegan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 266, 'width': 268, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-finnegan/09insidelist-finnegan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 266, 'width': 268, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-finnegan/09insidelist-finnegan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-finnegan/09insidelist-finnegan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/09/books/review/09insidelist-finnegan/09insidelist-finnegan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2015-07-31T15:19:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/417a5f3e-7f01-5ab7-b436-cda171b9ffec', 'word_count': 496, 'uri': 'nyt://article/417a5f3e-7f01-5ab7-b436-cda171b9ffec'}, {'abstract': 'It can easily feel as if no one’s bank account or credit card is safe. But for consumers, the effect is quite different from what the headlines suggest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/02/business/stolen-consumer-data-is-a-smaller-problem-than-it-seems.html', 'snippet': 'It can easily feel as if no one’s bank account or credit card is safe. But for consumers, the effect is quite different from what the headlines suggest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At Target, 40 million customers had their credit card information exposed to hackers. At JPMorgan Chase, personal details associated with 80 million accounts were leaked. Last month, a hacker gained access to 4.5 million records from the University of California, Los Angeles, health system.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/upshot/02up-view/02up-view-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/02/upshot/02up-view/02up-view-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/upshot/02up-view/02up-view-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 533, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/upshot/02up-view/02up-view-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1067, 'width': 2048, 's