aniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5f8b9cd-2418-55b0-9457-5358deed8b01', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5f8b9cd-2418-55b0-9457-5358deed8b01'}, {'abstract': 'Sara Paretsky’s crusading private eye, V.I. (Vic) Warshawski, returns in “Brush Back.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2015/08/02/books/review/flight-or-fright.html', 'snippet': 'Sara Paretsky’s crusading private eye, V.I. (Vic) Warshawski, returns in “Brush Back.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Don’t listen to Thomas Wolfe. You can always go home again — if you’re prepared to have your beating heart ripped from your chest and stomped on. Sara Paretsky’s crusading private eye, V.I. (Vic) Warshawski, finds that out in BRUSH BACK (Putnam, $27.95) when she lets a high school boyfriend talk her into returning to the ramshackle neighborhood on the South Side of Chicago where they grew up. Frank Guzzo, a truck driver for a haulage company, is worried about his unstable mother, Stella, a truculent old bat who just got out of prison after doing 25 years for beating her teenage daughter to death. (“She was getting those big ideas, way above herself.”) But when Vic pays an unannounced visit to Stella, who now claims to have been innocent, this harridan slanders Vic’s mother and blames the killing on the sleuth’s beloved cousin and Chicago hockey legend, Boom-Boom Warshawski (who was murdered far too early in this series). ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/0802-BKS-Crime/0802-BKS-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 795, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2015/08/02/books/review/0802-BKS-Crime/0802-BKS-Crime-a